1.e4 { take the center } {[%cal Ge2e4]} 1...e5 2.Nc3 { The vienna } 2...Nf6 ( 2...d6 3.Bc4 { Develop in a Vienna-like setup. } 3...Nf6 ( 3...c6 4.d3 Nf6 ( 4...b5 5.Bb3 a5 6.a4 b4 7.Nce2 { Black has locked the center, so we have to develop our pieces in a slightly cagey, flexible way, but it’s totally fine. He will always keep the …d5 pawn break in reserve to open the center if we go all‑in on a kingside attack, so for now just keep developing, try to discourage or control …d5, and remember: if he ever plays …c5, then a big kingside attack becomes much more dangerous for him because …d5 will be very hard to achieve. } ) 5.f4 exf4 6.Bxf4 { This is all standard Vienna stuff: the only special thing is that our opponent is preparing …d5, but we already have three pieces developed against his one, so breaking open the center is clearly in our favor. A key principle is that if you’re ahead in development, you usually want to open the position, so if Black hits with …d5 here, central tension and trades help us much more than him. } 6...d5 7.exd5 cxd5 8.Bb3 d4 ( 8...Bb4 { This pins your knight with the idea of winning it with d4, but you shouldn’t defend with fear. Play more aggressively than your opponent and answer the pin with active counterplay. Often the best “defense” against such pressure is to create your own threats, forcing them to respond instead of calmly executing their plan. } 9.Qe2+ Be6 10.Nf3 d4 { It might look like we blundered, but the sharp eye of a LanckrietChess subscriber knows exactly what’s going on under the surface. } ( 10...O-O 11.O-O d4 12.Ne4 { We have very active pieces and open lines; just don’t blunder your rooks on e1 and calmly pile up on the isolated d‑pawn. Use your rooks and minor pieces to attack that pawn from multiple directions, since an isolated pawn is a long‑term weakness you can squeeze without taking big risks } ) 11.Bxe6 fxe6 ( 11...dxc3 12.Bc4+ Qe7 13.Qxe7+ Kxe7 14.O-O-O cxb2+ 15.Kxb2 ) ( 11...Bxc3+ 12.bxc3 fxe6 13.Qxe6+ Qe7 14.Qxe7+ Kxe7 15.cxd4 b5 16.Rb1 ) 12.Qxe6+ Qe7 ( 12...Kf8 13.a3 Bxc3+ 14.bxc3 { Our threat is Bd6. } 14...Nc6 15.Bd6+ Ne7 16.Ne5 Qe8 { Stopping Qf7 mate. } 17.O-O Rd8 18.Rxf6+ gxf6 19.Rf1 Rxd6 20.Qxd6 Kg8 21.Qe6+ Kg7 22.Qxf6+ Kg8 23.Qe6+ Kg7 24.Rf7+ Kg8 25.Rxe7+ Kf8 26.Rf7+ Kg8 27.Qxe8# ) 13.Qxe7+ Kxe7 14.Nxd4 Nc6 15.Nxc6+ bxc6 16.a3 Bxc3+ 17.bxc3 Nd5 18.Be5 Rhf8 19.O-O-O { We have a bishop‑for‑knight advantage and we are up two pawns; we don’t have a concrete win yet, but the plan is simple: activate all your pieces and push your 3‑on‑1 central majority together with your king. Use the bishop’s long range and a centralized king to support the advancing central pawns, and only look for tactics once your pieces are maximally active. } ) 9.Qe2+ Be7 10.Ne4 { You are safe and have very active pieces; keep developing and increasing pressure on the isolated d‑pawn. You can castle either way, but since we already have a clear, positional target (the d‑pawn) and don’t need a wild king attack, castling kingside is usually a bit safer and more practical here. } ) ( 3...f5 4.exf5 Bxf5 5.Nf3 { You probably thought about going d3 and f4, leading to a Vienna‑esque structure, but in this position we actually have something even better than the “standard” Vienna setup. } 5...Nf6 6.O-O Nc6 7.d4 { We have more development and his king is still in the center, so we should strike the center open. When you’re ahead in development against an uncastled king, opening lines in the center greatly increases the power of your pieces and makes his king a long‑term target. } 7...exd4 8.Re1+ Be7 9.Nd5 Nxd5 10.Bxd5 Kf8 11.Bxc6 bxc6 12.Nxd4 Bd7 13.Qf3+ { We are winning at least a pawn here, and we also wish Black a lot of luck ever developing that poor rook on h8. } 13...Bf6 14.Nxc6 Bxc6 15.Qxc6 Rb8 16.Be3 Rxb2 17.Rab1 Rxa2 18.Rb7 ) 4.d3 Be7 5.f4 exf4 ( 5...O-O 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.f5 { Our plan is to start a pawn storm, and that’s exactly why we just locked the center: an attack on the side is strongest when the center is closed or firmly under control. which is classic middlegame wisdom. Yes it appears in an opening course. You're welcome. } 7...Na5 8.Bg5 Nxc4 9.dxc4 { Yes, we doubled our pawn structure, and before reading the explanation you should first think why this might actually be good. Once you’ve thought about it, it links straight back to the previous lesson: our c4 and e4 pawns clamp down on any central counterblow, so the center is essentially locked forever and we’re free to go all‑in with a pawn storm on the side. We’ll castle queenside and pawnstorm on the kingside, and because there is no real central counterplay, our “weak” doubled pawns are actually part of a very strong strategic setup. } 9...a6 { There is no counterplay in the center, so Black’s best practical try is to look for play on the queenside. With the center locked, the textbook rule says: the side attacking on the flank wants it to stay closed, while the defender must seek counterplay on the opposite wing or by breaking the pawn chain. } 10.Bxf6 Bxf6 11.Qd2 Bd7 12.O-O-O b5 13.g4 Qb8 14.g5 Bd8 15.Nd5 f6 16.Rhg1 bxc4 17.gxf6 Bxf6 18.Rg3 c6 ( 18...Ra7 19.Nxf6+ Rxf6 20.Rxg7+ Kxg7 21.Qg5+ Kf7 22.Nxe5+ dxe5 23.Rxd7+ Ke8 24.Qxf6 Kxd7 25.Qe6+ Kd8 26.Qg8+ Kd7 27.Qxb8 { To sac your rook five moves ago, you need to know that the idea of taking the queen and then trapping the rook even exists in the position; you won’t find it by brute‑force calculation alone. That’s why I pack in so many sample lines—not for you to memorize move orders, but so you internalize the patterns, plans, and threats and can recognize them instantly when they appear over the board, even if it is in a other situation. } ) 19.Nxf6+ Rxf6 20.Nxe5 dxe5 21.Qxd7 Qf8 22.Qb7 Re8 23.Rd7 Rf7 24.Rxf7 Qxf7 25.Qxa6 ) 6.Bxf4 O-O 7.Nf3 Bg4 8.h3 { All of these moves should come very naturally and without a thought; this is all pure Vienna‑like development. } 8...Bh5 9.g4 Bg6 10.Qe2 { Here we have the simple plan of queenside castling and pawn‑storming him. I wouldn’t be afraid of playing O‑O‑O followed by h4 and h5, even if it means giving up the g‑pawn. It is very hard to save the bishop, and we open up very important lines for our strong rooks. If he wastes time not getting the bishop trapped and grabbing the g‑pawn, he should be ill‑equipped to defend his king.
​ } 10...Nc6 11.a3 { This move pacifies the knight and makes sure that Ka5 can be met with Ba2. It is not the most aggressive line, but a crucial one for our devious continuations. } 11...Na5 ( 11...a6 12.O-O-O b5 { Yes, he can counterattack us, but don’t be scared. } 13.Bb3 { We put our bishop on b3 instead of a2 because we are provoking Na5; it looks like we waste a tempo, but in reality the knight lands badly on the rim in front of its own pawn storm. This way the knight blocks his queenside pawn majority, loses central influence, and makes it much easier for our passed c‑ and d‑pawns to run. } 13...Na5 14.Ba2 b4 15.axb4 Nc6 16.h4 h5 17.gxh5 Nxh5 18.Bd2 Nxb4 19.Bc4 c6 { Preparing d5. } 20.Rdg1 d5 21.exd5 cxd5 22.Nxd5 { Very difficult to spot, but you can sac your knight and there is no way for Black not to lose a piece back. Go through all of the sample lines until you are confident that you understand the tactic and can find it even in a completely different situation. It is a hard one; it is a combination of overloading the queen, an in‑between move,forks and various threats. } 22...Nxd5 23.Ne5 Rc8 ( 23...Kh7 24.Nc6 Qd6 25.Bxd5 Qxd5 26.Nxe7 Rfe8 27.Nxd5 Rxe2 28.Kd1 Rf2 ) ( 23...Qd6 24.Nxg6 fxg6 25.Rxg6 { Yes, we even sac the rook. We are two brilliant moves and a great move in. Are you not entertained? } 25...Qxg6 26.Bxd5+ Kh8 27.Bxa8 Bd6 ( 27...Rxa8 28.Qxe7 { We are up two pawns, which are already passed pawns; this makes converting very easy. } ) 28.Qg2 ) 24.Nxg6 fxg6 25.Qe6+ Kh7 26.Qxd5 Qxd5 27.Bxd5 { We are up a pawn, have the bishop pair, and a better structure. This is easy: we can either keep attacking to win more, trade down or create additional win conditions, or simply start exploiting our current win conditions (the passed c‑ and d‑pawns). } ) 12.Ba2 c5 13.h4 Nxg4 ( 13...h5 14.gxh5 Nxh5 15.Nd5 Nxf4 { This follows traditional wisdom: when attacked, trade pieces. But it is not that simple; it trades a key defender while bringing our knight closer, so it is actually quite bad. } 16.Nxf4 Kh8 { Getting out of the pin so that the bishop on g6 is not tactically hanging anymore. } 17.h5 Bh7 18.Ng6+ { Beautiful sacrifice; calculate all of the sample lines before you watch it to see its beauty for yourself first. } 18...fxg6 19.hxg6 c4 20.Rxh7+ Kg8 21.Qh2 Bh4+ 22.Nxh4 Rf1+ 23.Ke2 Re1+ 24.Rxe1 Qg5 25.Rh8+ Kxh8 26.Nf5+ Kg8 27.Qh7+ Kf8 28.Qh8# ) 14.h5 { Woeps! } ) ( 2...Bc5 3.Bc4 Nf6 ( 3...d6 4.Na4 Bb6 5.Nxb6 axb6 6.d3 Nc6 7.Ne2 { This move stops Nd4 but keeps the f‑pawn mobile, so we still have the option to launch f‑pawn breaks or kingside expansion later. } 7...Nf6 8.O-O O-O 9.h3 { We have to strengthen g4 before playing f4; otherwise we create way too big a gap in our defenses, which is easily exploitable around our king. } 9...h6 10.f4 exf4 11.Nxf4 Na5 12.Bd5 Nxd5 { Big mistake by Black: we have at least b4, trapping the knight, and in some lines it even leads straight to checkmate. } ( 12...c6 13.b4 cxd5 14.exd5 Re8 15.bxa5 Rxa5 16.Bd2 Nxd5 17.Bxa5 Ne3 18.Qb1 Nxf1 19.Bxb6 Nd2 20.Bxd8 Nxb1 21.Rxb1 Rxd8 22.Rb6 d5 23.Rb5 Be6 24.Rxb7 Ra8 25.Rb2 g5 26.Ne2 Kg7 27.Kf2 ) 13.exd5 Qf6 14.Rb1 Re8 ( 14...b5 15.b4 Nc6 16.dxc6 bxc6 17.a3 Bb7 18.Bb2 Qg5 19.Nh5 c5 20.Rf2 cxb4 21.Bxg7 bxa3 22.Bf6 Qg6 23.Rxb5 a2 24.Ba1 Rfb8 25.Qe1 Ba6 26.Qc3 Kf8 27.Rxb8+ Rxb8 28.Re2 Rb1+ 29.Kh2 f6 30.Qxc7 Bb5 31.Qd8+ Be8 32.Qe7+ Kg8 33.Nxf6+ Kh8 34.Qf8+ Qg8 35.Qxg8# ) 15.Ne6 Qe7 16.Nxg7 Kxg7 17.Qh5 Rh8 18.Bd2 Bd7 19.Rbe1 Qf8 20.Bc3+ f6 21.Rxf6 Kg8 22.Qg6+ Qg7 23.Rf8+ Kxf8 24.Qxg7# ) ( 3...Nc6 { This is called the copycat: Black is mirroring our moves, which happens a lot on the lower end of the Elo ladder, but it’s actually very good for us. As soon as we play a move he cannot safely copy, the symmetry breaks and his position already a bit worse, suddenly lands in a lot of trouble. } 4.Qg4 { We’re breaking several opening rules with this move, but if you know your lines, you have my blessing. We’re exploiting the weakness of g7 after the bishop moves to c5. There are three possible responses, but each of them is flawed in its own way. } 4...Qf6 { This looks like the most promising line because you both defend and attack f2. } ( 4...g6 5.Qf3 Nf6 ( 5...Qf6 { Don’t take and develop his pieces, let him take and develop our pieces instead. } 6.Nd5 Qxf3 7.Nxf3 Bb6 ( 7...Bd6 8.O-O { This is a weird maneuver I faced once, Black tries to defend c7 without giving us the chance to take his bishop. Our knight is strong, and the pressure on f7 is great. Just keep activating your pieces and adding more pressure. His bishop is basically a pawn with a funny hat, and it even blocks his own d-pawn, which shows really poor coordination. } ) 8.c3 d6 9.a4 Be6 10.Ng5 { Black has to be careful not to get his bishop trapped. There’s also the threat of Nxb6, Bxc4, and Nxc4, which would leave us up a piece. But even if that doesn’t happen, we can always take the bishop pair and open up the board so that our bishops become stronger than his knights. That way, we can win positionally if none of our tactics work out. } ) 6.Nge2 { This is a very important move, one you’ll easily forget if you only memorize the lines. If your queen is on f3 and his knight is on c6, you have to be careful about Nd4, which attacks both the queen and the very weak c2 pawn. After Qd1 you’re safe, but you’ve lost time and all your pressure is gone. That’s why this move is so important, it prevents Nd4. } 6...d6 ( 6...O-O 7.d3 Nd4 8.Nxd4 exd4 9.Bg5 Be7 10.Nb5 { We’re threatening Nxc7 Qxc7, Bxf6 Bxf6, Qxf6 ..., going up a pawn and reaching an incredible position.
Black is under pressure on the flank, so it shouldn’t be a surprise that there’s a very sharp line involving a central strike. } 10...d5 11.exd5 Bg4 12.Qg3 Bd7 13.O-O Bxb5 14.Bxb5 Nh5 15.Bxe7 Nxg3 16.Bxd8 Ne2+ ( 16...Nxf1 { It looks like Black wins the exchange, but actually the knight will be trapped. And when you try to counterattack our bishop, it will be defended in a beautiful way. Take a moment to think about the position and calculate for yourself, if you’re still tempted to take the knight, I don’t know what to say! There’s something truly devious hidden in this position. } 17.Bxc7 { We save our bishop by taking the pawn, so now Black has to focus on saving their knight. } 17...Nd2 { If we were to attack the knight, it would be trapped. So Black’s only hope is to counterattack our bishop and hope for a trade. But we’ve actually achieved a beautiful passed d-pawn; ironically, thanks to Black’s sacrifice in the center while looking for counterplay. We can now push our passed pawn, defend our bishop, and the knight will be lost. We’ll have two bishops for a rook and an incredible passed pawn. } 18.d6 a6 19.Bd7 f5 20.Rd1 Rad8 { I really want you to notice that if his king weren’t on a light square and his knight weren’t trapped, this position would be very close to a draw. After bishop takes rook and rook takes bishop, we’d have to lose our d-pawn, if not for Be6+ followed by d7. So, if bishop takes rook and rook takes bishop, and then the bishop moves without giving a check and the rook takes on d6, we’d only be up a pawn. Luckily, we have the check, and the knight is still trapped and will eventually be won. } 21.Bxd8 Rxd8 22.Be6+ Kg7 { Here we have one big problem: if we play d7, then Kf6 follows, and our bishop can’t both save itself and protect the pawn. So in this sample line, with a pawn on f5, we have to give up the d-pawn. But we can take the knight, preventing it from trading itself for a pawn, so technically, we save ourselves a pawn in the future… kind of. } 23.Rxd2 Rxd6 24.Bb3 { We’re still up a pawn and a bishop, so just activate the rook, trade the rooks if possible, and don’t allow any rook infiltration. Remember what I always tell you, you might have a winning position, but you don’t have a win condition yet. Create a passed pawn, and you’ll win this easily, 10 out of 10 times. } ) 17.Kh1 Raxd8 18.g3 Rxd5 19.Bc4 Re5 20.f4 Rh5 21.Kg2 Re8 22.h4 b6 23.Kf2 Re3 24.Rae1 Nxg3 25.Rxe3 Nxf1 26.Re8+ Kg7 27.Kxf1 Rxh4 28.Re4 ) 7.d3 { We reach a typical Vienna position, all the usual plans apply. We can castle on either side, develop our pieces, and play for a pawn storm or f4. } 7...Bg4 ( 7...h6 8.Bd2 ) 8.Qg3 Bxe2 ( 8...Qd7 9.Qh4 Nh5 ( 9...Ng8 10.Bd2 ) 10.f3 Be6 11.g4 ) ( 8...Be6 9.Bxe6 fxe6 10.Bh6 Qe7 11.Na4 ) 9.Nxe2 { We have to take with the knight to still stop Nd4 and keep our castling rights. } ) ( 4...Kf8 { This is the best move, but no one ever finds it. If your opponent does, it means he prepared this position. But if he actually prepared this position, he probably wouldn’t go for it in the first place. So either it’s a lucky strike, or he hates his position and just booted up Stockfish. } { All the same ideas apply, just go through the lines. It leads to similar positions, only with an awkward king. } 5.Qf3 Nf6 ( 5...Qf6 6.Nd5 Qxf3 ( 6...Nd4 7.Nxf6 Nxf3+ 8.Nxf3 gxf6 9.c3 ) 7.Nxf3 Bb6 ( 7...Bd6 8.O-O ) 8.a4 ) 6.Nge2 d6 7.h3 Be6 8.Bb3 ) 5.Nd5 Qxf2+ 6.Kd1 { We have the threat of Qxg7, trapping the rook. We also have the threat of Nxc7, forking the king and rook. There are threats involving the bishop, queen, and rook all lining up on the f7 pawn, and even the possibility of trapping the queen with Nh3 Qd4, d3 ... , c3. And there’s a lot more! Really take the time to learn the sample lines so you know which moves blunder which threats. If you know all the continuations, you’ll win every time you reach this position, and trust me, you’ll get here a lot. } 6...Kf8 ( 6...g6 7.Nh3 Qd4 ( 7...d6 8.Nxf2 Bxg4+ 9.Nxg4 ) 8.d3 d6 ( 8...Bb6 9.Rf1 d6 ( 9...Qc5 10.Nxb6 axb6 11.Bxf7+ ) 10.Qf3 ) 9.Qf3 Bxh3 10.Rf1 ) ( 6...Nf6 7.Qxg7 Nxd5 8.Qxh8+ Ke7 9.Qg8 ) ( 6...d6 7.Qxg7 Nd4 8.Nxc7+ Kd8 9.Nf3 Kxc7 ( 9...Nxf3 10.Rf1 Qh4 11.Nd5 Qh6 12.Qxh8 Qg6 13.Rxf3 Bg4 14.c3 Bxf3+ 15.gxf3 Qg1+ 16.Kc2 ) 10.Rf1 Ne6 11.Qxf7+ ) 7.Nh3 Qd4 ( 7...h5 8.Qg5 Qd4 ( 8...f6 9.Qg6 Rh6 10.Ne7 Rxg6 ( 10...Kxe7 11.Qxg7+ Kd6 12.Nxf2 ) 11.Nxg6+ Ke8 12.Nxf2 Bxf2 13.Bxg8 ) 9.d3 Be7 10.Qg3 ) 8.d3 d6 9.Qf3 Bxh3 ( 9...h5 10.Ke1 Bg4 11.Qf1 ) 10.Rf1 Be6 11.c3 ) 4.d3 O-O 5.f4 d6 ( 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 exf4 ( 6...Ng4 7.Qe2 { Don’t get forked! } 7...Nf2 ( 7...Bf2+ 8.Kf1 Nd4 9.Nxd4 exd4 { He has to recapture with the pawn; otherwise his knight is simply hanging and he has no threat of equal value to compensate. } { Or at least that’s what he thinks—in reality this is a blunder. His best line was to get the bishop out; we cannot just capture the knight because …d5 would come, winning our bishop back, so sacrificing the knight for …d5 was actually his best defensive idea. } ( 9...Bxd4 10.Qxg4 d5 11.f5 dxc4 12.Nd5 c6 ( 12...a6 { This is not a natural move, but I just want to show you our threats so that you understand this position better. } 13.Bh6 g6 14.c3 Ba7 15.Qg3 cxd3 16.Rd1 Bd7 17.Bxf8 Bb5 18.Ke1 Qxf8 19.Nxc7 Rd8 20.Nxb5 axb5 21.Rxd3 ) 13.Bh6 g6 14.Nc3 cxd3 15.cxd3 Re8 16.h4 ) 10.h3 dxc3 11.hxg4 Bd4 12.bxc3 Bxc3 13.Rb1 { We get incredibly active pieces and a beautiful position out of this. } ) 8.Rf1 Ng4 9.f5 Nf6 10.Bg5 Be7 11.O-O-O { Pawnstorm time: attack the king, you know the drill, these are the typical plans here. With a closed center and a space advantage on the kingside, we just roll our pawns forward to open lines toward his king, bringing pieces behind them to finish the job. } ) 7.Bxf4 Re8 8.Ng5 Rf8 9.Rf1 d6 10.Qd2 Bb4 11.O-O-O { Expand in the center or pawnstorm: attack the king, you know the drill, these are the typical plans here. With a closed center and a space advantage on the kingside, we just roll our pawns forward to open lines toward his king, bringing pieces behind them to finish the job. } 11...Bd7 12.d4 h6 13.Nxf7 Rxf7 14.Bxf7+ Kxf7 15.e5 dxe5 16.dxe5 Nh7 17.Bg5+ ) ( 5...d5 6.Nxd5 Be6 { This is perfect play for Black: he accepts a structural weakness but gets good piece activity, while we get the strong central pawn mass but are stuck with a bad bishop behind our own pawns. Our plan will usually be to use our central control and space to slowly improve the bishop (or trade it), while Black tries to use his activity and our weak squares as dynamic compensation for his pawn weakness. } 7.Nxf6+ Qxf6 8.Bxe6 fxe6 9.Nf3 exf4 10.d4 Bb6 11.a4 Nc6 12.c3 a5 13.e5 Qf5 14.Bd2 Ne7 15.Qb3 Qe4+ 16.Kf2 Qf5 17.Rhf1 Rad8 18.Kg1 h6 19.Rae1 Kh7 20.h3 g5 21.g4 ) 6.Nf3 { Standard Vienna development. } 6...Bb4 7.f5 Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 Bd7 9.O-O { We have a clear space advantage and a basically closed center, which in classical strategy usually points toward a kingside attack. With more room on that side, our pieces maneuver more easily than his, so we can build up slowly and launch a pawn storm while his army is cramped and short of good defensive squares. } ) ( 2...Bb4 3.d4 { This is a rare move, but it leads to pawn structures very similar to the main lines of the gambit, so the typical plans and ideas you learned there still apply. } 3...Bxc3+ ( 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 Bxc3+ 5.Qxc3 Nf6 6.Be3 Nxe4 ( 6...Qe7 7.f3 Nc6 8.O-O-O ) 7.Qe5+ { woeps! } 7...Qe7 8.Qxg7 Qf6 9.Qg4 Qe6 10.Qh4 Nc6 11.O-O-O d5 12.f3 Ng3 13.Re1 Nf5 ( 13...Nxh1 14.Bf4 ) 14.Qf4 O-O 15.Bf2 Qd6 16.Nh3 ) ( 3...d6 4.dxe5 dxe5 ( 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 dxe5 6.Qxd8+ Kxd8 7.Bc4 f6 8.f4 exf4 9.Bxf4 Bd7 10.O-O-O Ne7 11.e5 Ng6 12.exf6 Nxf4 ( 12...gxf6 13.Bh6 ) 13.fxg7 Re8 14.Bf7 Nh5 15.Bxe8 Nxg7 16.Bxd7 Nxd7 ) 5.Qxd8+ Kxd8 6.Bd2 Nf6 7.O-O-O { His king is stuck in the center, so just develop fast and pile on the pressure against the middle. } 7...Ke7 8.Re1 Rd8 9.a3 Bd6 10.f4 exf4 11.e5 ) ( 3...Nc6 4.dxe5 Nxe5 5.Qd4 Bxc3+ 6.Qxc3 d6 7.Nf3 Nxf3+ 8.gxf3 ) 4.bxc3 exd4 5.cxd4 d6 6.Bd3 Ne7 7.Ne2 O-O 8.O-O ) ( 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 Bc5 { This is called the copycat: Black is mirroring our moves, which happens a lot on the lower end of the Elo ladder, but it’s actually very good for us. As soon as we play a move he cannot safely copy, the symmetry breaks and his position already a bit worse, suddenly lands in a lot of trouble. } ( 3...Nf6 4.d3 Na5 ( 4...Bb4 5.Ne2 d5 ( 5...O-O 6.O-O h6 7.a3 Bxc3 ( 7...Ba5 8.f4 ) ( 7...Bc5 8.b4 Bb6 9.Na4 d6 10.Nxb6 axb6 11.f4 ) 8.Nxc3 d6 9.f4 ) 6.exd5 Nxd5 7.O-O Bxc3 ( 7...Nxc3 8.bxc3 Bd6 9.Ng3 O-O 10.a4 ) ( 7...Be6 8.a3 Be7 ( 8...Bxc3 9.bxc3 O-O 10.a4 ) 9.Bxd5 Bxd5 10.f4 O-O 11.f5 Nd4 12.f6 Bxf6 13.Rxf6 Qxf6 ( 13...gxf6 14.Nxd4 exd4 15.Bh6 dxc3 ( 15...Kh8 16.Nxd5 Qxd5 17.Qf1 Rg8 ( 17...Qe5 18.Re1 Qd6 19.Bxf8 Rxf8 ) ( 17...Qc6 18.Bxf8 Rxf8 ) 18.Qxf6+ Rg7 19.Qxg7# ) 16.Qg4+ Kh8 17.Qg7# ) 14.Nxd5 ) 8.Nxc3 Nxc3 9.bxc3 O-O 10.Qf3 h6 11.Ba3 ) ( 4...Bc5 5.f4 d6 ( 5...exf4 6.Bxf4 d6 7.Nf3 ) ( 5...O-O ) 6.Nf3 Bg4 ( 6...Ng4 7.Ng5 Nf2 ( 7...h6 8.f5 hxg5 9.Qxg4 f6 10.h4 Nd4 11.Rh3 Nxc2+ 12.Kd1 Nxa1 13.hxg5 Rxh3 14.Qxh3 ) ( 7...O-O 8.f5 Nf2 ( 8...Bf2+ 9.Kf1 Ne3+ 10.Bxe3 Bxe3 11.h4 Bxg5 12.hxg5 Qxg5 13.Rh5 Qf4+ 14.Kg1 Qe3+ 15.Kh1 Nd4 16.Nd5 Qe2 17.c3 Qxd1+ 18.Rxd1 Ne2 19.Rh4 g5 20.Nf6+ Kg7 21.Nh5+ Kh6 ( 21...Kh8 22.Rh2 Nf4 23.Nf6 Kg7 ( 23...h5 24.g3 Kg7 25.gxf4 Kxf6 26.fxg5+ Kxg5 27.Rg1+ Kf6 ( 27...Kh6 28.Bxf7 Rxf7 29.Rg6+ Kh7 30.Rxh5# ) 28.Rxh5 ) 24.Nxh7 Rh8 25.Nxg5 Rxh2+ 26.Kxh2 f6 27.Nh3 ) 22.Rh2 g4 23.Ng3+ Kg5 24.Nxe2 ) ( 8...Nf6 9.Nd5 Nxd5 10.Bxd5 h6 11.Qh5 hxg5 12.Bxg5 Ne7 ( 12...Qe8 13.f6 g6 ( 13...Bf5 14.exf5 Qd7 15.g4 Nd4 16.fxg7 Qxf5 17.gxf5 Kxg7 18.Qh6+ Kg8 19.Qg6+ Kh8 20.Bf6# ) ( 13...Bf2+ ) 14.Qxg6+ Kh8 15.Qg7# ) 13.f6 Nxd5 14.fxg7 f6 ( 14...Kxg7 15.Qh6+ Kg8 16.Bxd8 Rxd8 17.exd5 Bf5 18.Rf1 Bg6 19.h4 Rd7 20.h5 Bh7 21.Rf3 e4 22.Rg3+ Bg6 23.hxg6 f5 24.g7 ) 15.exd5 fxg5 ( 15...Qe7 16.Bxf6 Qxf6 ( 16...Rxf6 17.Qh8+ Kf7 18.g8=Q# ) 17.Qh8+ Kf7 18.gxf8=Q+ Kg6 19.Qfxf6# ) 16.Qh8+ Kf7 17.gxf8=Q+ Qxf8 18.Rf1+ Bf5 19.Rxf5+ Kg6 20.Rxf8 Rxf8 21.Qxf8 Be3 22.Ke2 Bf4 23.g3 e4 24.gxf4 gxf4 25.Rg1+ Kh7 26.Qg7# ) 9.Qh5 h6 10.Bxf7+ Kh8 11.Qg6 hxg5 12.Qh5# ) 8.Qh5 g6 9.Bxf7+ Kd7 ( 9...Ke7 10.Nd5+ Kd7 11.Be6+ Ke8 12.Qh6 Nd4 13.Qg7 Bxe6 14.Nxe6 Nxe6 15.Nf6+ Qxf6 16.Qxf6 Kd7 17.Qf7+ Kc6 18.Qxe6 Nxh1 19.fxe5 Rae8 20.Qd5+ Kd7 21.exd6 Bxd6 22.Bg5 ) 10.Be6+ Ke8 11.Qh6 Nd4 12.Qg7 Bxe6 13.Qxh8+ Kd7 14.Qxh7+ Kc8 15.Nxe6 Nxe6 16.Rf1 ) ( 6...O-O 7.f5 h6 ( 7...Nd4 8.Nd5 Nxd5 9.Bxd5 c6 10.Bb3 Nxb3 11.axb3 h6 12.Qe2 Bd7 13.Be3 Bxe3 14.Qxe3 Qe7 15.O-O ) 8.Nd5 Nxd5 9.Bxd5 ) ( 6...exf4 7.Bxf4 O-O ( 7...Bg4 8.Qd2 ) ( 7...Be6 8.Bb5 a6 9.Bxc6+ bxc6 10.Qd2 Rb8 ) 8.d4 Bb4 ( 8...Bb6 9.Bg5 h6 10.Bh4 g5 11.Bf2 ) 9.Bg5 ) 7.Na4 Nd4 ( 7...Bb6 8.h3 Bxf3 9.Qxf3 Nd4 10.Qf2 Ba5+ ( 10...O-O 11.fxe5 dxe5 12.c3 Nb3 13.Nxb6 Nxa1 14.Nxa8 Qxa8 15.O-O ) 11.c3 b5 12.O-O bxc4 13.cxd4 exd4 14.Qxd4 cxd3 15.e5 ) 8.c3 Nxf3+ 9.gxf3 Bd7 10.Nxc5 dxc5 11.fxe5 ) 5.Qf3 Nxc4 ( 5...c6 6.Nge2 b5 7.Bb3 ) 6.dxc4 d6 ( 6...Bb4 7.Ne2 d6 8.O-O O-O 9.h3 Be6 10.b3 c6 11.Rd1 ) ( 6...Bc5 7.Be3 Bxe3 8.Qxe3 O-O 9.O-O-O d6 10.h3 Be6 11.f4 exf4 ( 11...Bxc4 12.fxe5 ) 12.Qxf4 Bxc4 13.e5 ) 7.Bg5 Be7 8.O-O-O ) ) 3.f4 { Play the gambit line. Trust me, it’s very good. } 3...exf4 ( 3...Nc6 { It looks like a logical, principled move, “develop knights before bishops,” right?
Wrong. In this specific position it’s already almost lost(+1.6), and we basically get a Halloween Gambit on steroids. } 4.fxe5 Nxe5 5.d4 Ng6 ( 5...Nc6 6.e5 Ng8 ( 6...Qe7 7.Be2 { The d-pawn is attacked, so we cannot go for Qe2. } 7...Ng8 8.Nf3 { The knight from f has moved, so we have stopped Qh4+ as always. } ) 7.Nf3 { The knight from f has moved, so we have stopped Qh4+ as always. } 7...d6 8.Bb5 dxe5 ( 8...a6 9.Ba4 dxe5 10.Nxe5 Bd7 11.Nxd7 Qxd7 12.Qe2+ Qe7 13.Be3 O-O-O 14.Bxc6 bxc6 15.O-O-O Kb7 16.d5 c5 17.Rhe1 ) 9.Nxe5 { Put Pressure the pinned piece. PP on the PP. } 9...Bd7 10.Nxd7 { We have to maintain the pin. } 10...Qxd7 11.d5 { PP on the PP again. } 11...a6 12.Qe2+ Qe7 13.dxc6 axb5 14.cxb7 Qxe2+ 15.Kxe2 Rb8 16.a4 Rxb7 17.axb5 f5 18.Rd1 Nf6 19.Ra8+ Kf7 20.Bg5 Rg8 21.Bxf6 gxf6 22.Rd7+ Ke6 23.Rd3 Rxg2+ 24.Kf3 Rxc2 25.Rxf8 ) ( 5...Neg4 6.e5 { ince the knights are defending each other, you just have to remove one to win the other. } 6...Qe7 7.Qe2 ) 6.e5 { Expand in the center. } 6...Ng8 7.Nf3 d6 8.Bc4 dxe5 9.O-O { This sets up a trap. } 9...exd4 10.Ng5 dxc3 11.Bxf7+ Ke7 12.Qe2+ { Very important to give the check on e2 so that the rook can slide over. } 12...Kd6 13.Rd1+ ) ( 3...d6 4.Nf3 { Develop, putting pressure on the center. } 4...Nc6 ( 4...exf4 5.d4 { Take the center and attack the f-pawn. } 5...Qe7 { attacks the e-pawn. } ( 5...Nh5 6.Nd5 { Put more pressure on the f‑pawn; this is another sharp variation you really have to know. } 6...g5 7.Nxg5 { Sac the knight, this is what makes the line so sharp, but trust me, your opponent’s jaw will drop. } 7...Qxg5 8.Qxh5 { And this is why. } 8...Qxh5 9.Nf6+ Ke7 10.Nxh5 Nc6 11.c3 { We will go up a pawn and have incredible activity, which means the position is objectively winning for us.
If you know me as a teacher, this is the point where I tell you the difference between a winning position and a win condition.
We have a winning position; our job now is to create a win condition: either
a direct checkmate attack (which will be difficult here), or
a passed pawn, which is usually much easier to play,
winning the weak f-pawn and
finding a good pawn break that helps us generate a passed pawn.
That’s your quick endgame/conversion lesson: don’t stop at “better,” always convert your advantage into a concrete win condition. } ) 6.Bd3 Nxe4 { It looks like this sac works. } 7.Nxe4 d5 { This attacks a pinned piece with a pawn. PP on the PP. } 8.O-O dxe4 9.Bxe4 { For the cost of a pawn we have a very active bishop pair, it’s hard for Black to develop, we have a queenside majority, and on top of that we also have a trap in the position. } 9...Qxe4 ( 9...Qf6 { Tries to hold on to the pawn. } 10.Bxf4 Qxf4 11.Qe2 { This is a difficult move to remember, but you have to know this sharp variation. } 11...Be7 12.Ne5 Qh4 13.g3 Qh3 14.Nxf7 Rf8 15.Rae1 Qe6 16.Qh5 Rxf7 17.Bxh7 ) 10.Re1 { woeps! } ) ( 4...Bg4 5.h3 Bxf3 ( 5...Bh5 { This blunders a bishop. } 6.g4 Bg6 7.f5 ) ( 5...Bd7 { This wastes a move; he could have gone Bd7 in one go and not allowed us to improve our h‑pawn for free. } 6.Bc4 Nc6 ( 6...Be6 { It looks dumb at first sight: the bishop moves three times just to get traded off.

But this choice leads to very unique lines, and with some guidance you’ll understand the logic behind it and memorize it easily — you really need both understanding (the “why”) and concrete memorization (the exact moves). } 7.Bxe6 fxe6 8.d4 { This move looks weird at first, but when you’re up in activity you should strike in the center.

We have two knights developed against his one, plus a much better bishop thanks to the pawn color complex, so opening the center lets our better‑placed pieces do maximum damage. } 8...exd4 ( 8...exf4 9.Bxf4 Nh5 { This is the only line that kind of holds, engines might call it something like +1.5, but practically we’ll just get a dominant activity lead. } ( 9...Be7 { This move basically loses; we have way more activity, so opening the position now heavily favors our pieces and turns his slightly worse game into a strategically lost one. } 10.Ng5 Qd7 11.e5 dxe5 12.dxe5 { Usually you should never trade queens when you’re attacking or have a big lead in development.
​

But here, if our opponent takes, the queen trade comes with a bonus: it leads to the powerful development of our rook, so the resulting position actually keeps (or even increases) our initiative despite the queens coming off. } 12...Nd5 13.Nxd5 Qxd5 14.Qxd5 exd5 15.Ne6 { Fork! } 15...Kd7 16.Nxg7 Rg8 17.e6+ { This is a beautiful in‑between move: yes, our knight is hanging, but we first win a tempo before recapturing. } 17...Kd8 18.Nf5 Rxg2 { Sac the pawn. } 19.O-O-O { He cannot allow Rxd5. } 19...c6 20.Rhg1 { Take over the file — this is possible because of our activity: all of our pieces are useful, we already have a strong passed pawn (our win condition), while he has 8 points of material stuck and crying in the corner doing nothing. } 20...Rg6 { He cannot give us the file for free; if he allows our rook to fully control it without contest, our activity and passed pawn will decide the game almost by themselves. } 21.Rxg6 hxg6 22.Rg1 { This is a beautiful sacrifice — the position is completely lost for Black.

Even his best try, g5, is totally hopeless: our three active pieces versus his lonely bishop will overthrow his king, we’ll create two passed pawns, and stopping their promotion is practically impossible. } 22...gxf5 23.Rg8+ Bf8 24.Bd6 Nd7 25.e7+ Ke8 26.Rxf8+ Nxf8 27.exf8=Q+ Kd7 28.Qe7+ Kc8 29.Qc7# { His rook never even got to move. } ) 10.Bh2 Be7 11.O-O O-O 12.Qd3 { We just have more activity. } ) 9.Nxd4 e5 10.Ne6 Qd7 11.f5 { Our knight is way stronger than his bishop because of his pawn colour complex.

His pawns are stuck on the same colour as the bishop, making it a classic “bad bishop,” while our knight can hop onto an outpost and dominate both colours — so we should anchor it in. } 11...Na6 12.Qf3 Nc5 { He really should try to trade our knight off; otherwise it will be a huge thorn in his side for the rest of the game. } 13.Nd5 Nxe6 14.fxe6 { Black has to be very careful here; a lot of tactics go our way simply because of our superior activity.
You can have a look at the sample lines to see how the ideas play out in concrete variations and to reinforce the patterns we’ve been talking about. } 14...Qd8 ( 14...Qxe6 15.Nxc7+ ) 15.O-O Be7 16.Qg3 O-O { No human finds this, but it’s the only engine move that kind of holds for a brief moment longer. } 17.Bh6 Nh5 18.Qg4 Bf6 19.Rxf6 Rxf6 20.e7 Qe8 21.Nxf6+ ) ( 6...exf4 7.d4 { Take the center when it is given to you. } 7...Nh5 8.O-O Nc6 9.d5 { Expand with tempo by attacking the knight. } 9...Nb4 10.a3 { Bully the knight to a passive position. } 10...Na6 11.e5 { Break in the center to exploit our development lead and his king that is stuck in the center. We sacrificed a pawn, but both knights wasted a lot of moves just to become very passive on the side, so our activity advantage will be crushing. } 11...dxe5 12.Nxe5 Nf6 13.Bxf4 { He gives the pawn back just to defend, and we are still crushing and no longer even a pawn down. } 13...Bc5+ 14.Kh1 Bd6 15.Nxd7 Nxd7 16.Qe2+ Qe7 17.Rae1 Qxe2 18.Rxe2+ Kd8 19.Bxd6 cxd6 20.Bxa6 bxa6 21.Rxf7 { Trade everything off like this to damage his structure. Now we are up a pawn and have an activity lead.

If you know me as a teacher, this is the point where I explain the difference between a winning position and a win condition. We have a winning position, but we only have a win condition when we either checkmate our opponent, which will be difficult, or make our extra pawn a passed pawn. This is way easier because of our activity and his bad structure.

I would advise playing the c5 pawn break to turn our d‑pawn into a passed pawn, which will be our win condition. Here you go: a random endgame lesson in an opening course, only for the students who actually learn it deeply. } ) 7.Ng5 { Beautiful move: we attack the f7 pawn, and the only way to defend it is to move his bishop a third time and allow us to trade it off.
That gives us an incredible lead in development plus a small, but very dangerous, attacking initiative.
​
​ } 7...Be6 8.Bxe6 fxe6 9.Nxe6 Qe7 10.Nxf8 Rxf8 { Now he cannot castle short, and we have the only bishop. } 11.O-O exf4 { You could take back and be slightly better, but I recommend a sharper line that you’ll have to learn.
It gives you a bigger advantage and also makes you more familiar with important tactical and strategic patterns.
​ } 12.Nd5 Nxd5 13.exd5 Ne5 14.d4 Nc4 { We bully the knight to open up our pieces and gain space, but we still haven’t actually won our pawn back yet. } 15.Qd3 Ne3 16.Bxe3 Qxe3+ 17.Qxe3+ fxe3 18.Rae1 Rxf1+ 19.Kxf1 Kd7 { This will probably liquidate into a king‑and‑pawn endgame where we’re simply up a pawn and have the more active king.
Positions like that should be winning the vast majority of the time if you know basic king‑and‑pawn technique (opposition, key squares, and active king) — think something like 90% wins and only about 10% practical draws at club level.
If you struggle with endgames like this or you’re not confident in that 90–10 statistic, that’s exactly the kind of position you should study in a focused endgame course.
By the time you’re watching this, I might even have an endgame course out that drills these king‑and‑pawn techniques so you can convert “plus one pawn and active king” almost } ) 6.Qxf3 Nc6 ( 6...exf4 7.d4 g5 { He thinks he got away with taking the pawn and that we only got central control for the pawn, but central control cannot be underestimated, as you will see. } 8.e5 { We attack the knight, but even worse, who is defending the b‑pawn?
​ } 8...dxe5 9.dxe5 Nfd7 10.Qxb7 c6 { It looks like he is trying to trap our queen with Qxa8 Qc7, ... Nb6. } 11.Qxa8 { It is safe to take the rook if you know the lines, though. } 11...Qc7 12.Ba6 { Our idea is Ba6 Nb6, Qb7.
​ } 12...Nc5 { This stops our Qb7 plan. } 13.Bb5 { We have to sacrifice the bishop to open a diagonal for our queen.
We will go up the exchange and have a better structure. } 13...cxb5 14.O-O Nd7 15.Qe4 { We are up the exchange and have a queenside majority. This is a winning position without a win condition.

To create a win condition, we have to activate all of our pieces and start a majority attack until we get a passed pawn. We might be able to trade off our weak e‑pawn, but I would not recommend trading it for a doubled f‑pawn. Maybe it can serve as a battering ram to open up the king, or we win the f7‑pawn and turn our e‑pawn into our win condition. } ) ( 6...Be7 7.Bc4 O-O 8.O-O Nc6 9.d3 Nd4 10.Qd1 { Don’t blunder your queen and don’t blunder c2. We will just activate our pieces and attack his king, typical Vienna stuff. } ) 7.Bb5 { We are trying to damage his pawn structure again.
​ } ) 5.Bb5 { Develop, destabilising his center. } 5...Bd7 { He unpins to not lose a pawn, and no, Bxc6 doesn’t win a pawn, because after Bxc6 Bxc6, fxe5 dxe5, Nxe5, your e4 pawn is hanging. } ( 5...Bg4 6.h3 { Force him to make a decision. } 6...Bxf3 ( 6...Bh5 7.g4 Bg6 8.f5 ) 7.Qxf3 { We are trying to damage his pawn structure again.
​ } 7...Be7 8.Bxc6+ bxc6 9.fxe5 dxe5 10.O-O O-O 11.d3 { His pawn structure is bad, so this position is winning for us in the long run.

His only real plan is c5 and then c4 to trade one weakness, but after b3 that becomes impossible and our dark‑squared bishop will be very powerful, because all our pawns are on light squares. } ) ( 5...Be7 6.fxe5 dxe5 7.Nxe5 { Put preasure on the pinned piece, he will have to give us the bishop pair and we won a pawn. } ) ( 5...exf4 6.d4 Be7 7.d5 a6 8.Ba4 b5 9.Nxb5 axb5 10.Bxb5 O-O 11.Bxc6 Nxe4 12.O-O Rb8 13.Nd4 g5 14.c3 f5 15.a4 Bf6 16.Ne6 Bxe6 17.dxe6 Qc8 18.Qd5 Kg7 ) 6.d3 { Stabilising our center. } 6...exf4 ( 6...Nd4 7.Bxd7+ Qxd7 8.Nxd4 exd4 9.Ne2 c5 10.O-O Be7 11.f5 { We have a big space advantage on the kingside; castling there is suicide for Black because our pieces will swarm his king.

