1.e4 { take the center } {[%cal Ge2e4]} 1...c6 { most likely the caro-kann } 2.d4 { take the whole center if given to you } 2...d5 { now we are sure its the caro-khan } 3.exd5 { Theoretically speaking, the advanced variation is the best, but your opponent will be very comfortable.
We are smarter and play THY Lanckriet System, which constantly forces our opponent into very uncomfortable decisions. } 3...cxd5 { Our opponent wants a Caro-Kann, but we’ve got something devious prepared that’s going to make him very uncomfortable. } 4.Bd3 { We play against the bishop on c8.
As Magnus Carlsen said, “Pick a piece and play against it.”
We will make it very difficult for the bishop to develop.
Our bishop controls the f5 square, and our queen prevents Bg4. } 4...Nc6 ( 4...Nf6 5.h3 Nc6 6.c3 { Stop the plan. } ) 5.c3 { Dont blunder your d poin. } 5...Nf6 { This intends to prepare the development of the bishop to g4. } 6.h3 { Stop the plan. } 6...e5 { This fights for the center, opens it up, and ensures that the bishop does not get stuck behind a light-squared pawn chain. It is the best move, but very uncomfortable for a Caro-Kann player. } ( 6...e6 7.Nf3 { Our opponent had the chance to play e5 and didn’t take it. His best plan is either a minority attack on the queenside or eventually playing e5, so we’ll make that plan as difficult as possible. In some positions it’ll be impossible to stop e5 completely, but we’ll still end up with very nice positions. } 7...Bd6 ( 7...Qc7 8.Qe2 { Develop and try to discourage e5. } 8...Nb4 { Qe2 has the drawback that your c1 bishop is no longer defended. The move is tricky, but it’s not that good. } ( 8...Bd6 9.O-O O-O 10.Re1 { A lot of the e6 lines will eventually transpose to this setup. In this structure, don’t make the mistake of putting your queen on c2, and you should be able to launch some devious kingside attacks. } ) 9.Bb5+ Bd7 10.Bxd7+ { Yes, we give up our strong bishop for his basically French bishop, but we gain time by doing this. After castling, he’ll have to save the knight. Taking with the queen blunders a knight, and taking with the king gives up castling rights, so knight takes is forced, pulling a key defender away from the kingside. } 10...Nxd7 11.O-O { Now that we’ve defended our c1 bishop, he has to save his knight. } 11...Nc6 12.c4 { It’s a very difficult and sharp line, you’ll probably have to memorize this one. Of course, there is some logic behind the moves that will help you remember them. } 12...dxc4 13.Nc3 Nb4 14.d5 e5 15.Nb5 Qc5 16.Nxe5 Be7 ( 16...Qxb5 17.Nc6+ Be7 18.Qxe7# ) 17.a4 Nxe5 18.Qxe5 O-O 19.Be3 Qxd5 20.Qxe7 Nd3 21.Nc7 Qd8 22.Bg5 ) 8.O-O O-O 9.Re1 { We develop while stopping e5. } 9...Qc7 10.Qe2 { A lot of the e6 lines will eventually transpose to this setup. In this structure, don’t make the mistake of putting your queen on c2, and you should be able to launch some devious kingside attacks. } 10...Nd7 11.Be3 e5 { This is one of the variations where we can’t stop e5, but that’s fine. Our opponent wasted time by playing e6 and then e5. We can still give him an isolated queen’s pawn, and these positions will look quite similar to the lines where he plays e5 immediately. } ( 11...Re8 12.Nbd2 e5 { This is one of the variations where we can’t stop e5, but that’s fine. Our opponent wasted time by playing e6 and then e5. We can still give him an isolated queen’s pawn, and these positions will look quite similar to the lines where he plays e5 immediately. } ( 12...h6 13.c4 dxc4 14.Nxc4 Bb4 15.Rec1 Bd6 16.Nxd6 Qxd6 17.Qd2 { Yes, we have an IQP here, but we won the bishop pair in a very open position, which gave us a development lead and crushing activity. } 17...a6 { This is not the most natural move, but I just wanted to show you a threat so you understand this position better, and maybe I also wanted to show off the power and venom of this opening a little. } 18.Bxh6 gxh6 19.Qxh6 f5 20.Qg6+ Kf8 21.Ng5 Re7 22.Bxf5 Qxd4 23.Nxe6+ Rxe6 24.Bxe6 Qg7 25.Qf5+ Qf6 26.Rc5 ) ( 12...a6 13.c4 { Yes, we did give ourselves an IQP again, but we’ll have a nice attack on the queenside. That’s a rare plan, but it exists, and it’s one you need to understand.
​ } 13...dxc4 14.Nxc4 Bb4 15.Rec1 Nf6 16.Nfe5 Rd8 17.a3 Be7 18.Nxc6 bxc6 { We have two targets now. } 19.b4 { If your opponent has weaknesses, make sure they can’t trade them off, that’s why we play b4, to stop c5.
​ } 19...Nd5 20.Qf3 Bd7 21.Rc2 Nxe3 22.fxe3 Be8 23.Na5 c5 24.dxc5 Rxd3 ( 24...Bg5 25.Kh1 h6 26.Rcc1 ) 25.Qxa8 ) 13.dxe5 Ndxe5 14.Bc2 Be6 15.Ng5 h6 16.Nxe6 Rxe6 ( 16...fxe6 17.Rad1 Rad8 18.Bf4 Nf3+ ( 18...Nd7 19.Bxd6 Qxd6 20.Nf3 Nf6 21.Nh2 Qf4 22.Ng4 Nxg4 23.hxg4 g5 24.Rd3 Qf6 25.Rf3 Qg7 26.Re3 e5 27.Ba4 ) 19.Nxf3 Bxf4 20.Qd3 ) 17.Bb3 Ne7 18.Nf3 Nc4 19.Nd4 Re5 20.Nb5 Qc6 { We have a tactic here, the queen is overloaded. We only win the weak isolated queen’s pawn, but after that we’ll have a queenside majority, create a passed pawn, and win the game that way. } 21.Bxc4 dxc4 22.Nxd6 Qxd6 23.Qxc4 ) 12.dxe5 Ndxe5 13.Bc2 Be6 14.Nbd2 ) ( 6...Qc7 7.Nf3 { Develop a piece and try to discourage e5. } 7...e5 ( 7...e6 ) 8.dxe5 Nxe5 9.Qe2 { You could probably hope for a transposition with Bc2, but the pin is better and more dangerous. In a lot of variations our queen goes to e2 anyway, so why not do that now with some threats and tricks. } 9...Bd6 ( 9...Nd7 10.O-O { If Nxe5 and Nxe5, we win a piece, but he also has queen takes here, so it doesn’t work, just develop. } 10...Be7 11.Re1 Nxf3+ 12.Qxf3 Nf6 13.Bf4 Qb6 14.Na3 Qxb2 15.Nb5 { Nc7 is an unstoppable threat. } 15...O-O ( 15...Bd7 16.Rab1 Qxa2 17.Nc7+ Kd8 ( 17...Kf8 18.Nxa8 ) 18.Nxa8 Bc6 19.Bc7+ Kc8 20.Bf5+ Bd7 21.Rxe7 Bxf5 22.Qxf5+ Nd7 23.Qxd7# ) 16.Rab1 Qxa2 17.Rxe7 ) 10.Nxe5 Bxe5 11.f4 ) ( 6...g6 { This line has the unique idea of developing the bishop to f5. } 7.Bf4 { Develop a piece and try to discourage e5. } 7...Bf5 8.Nf3 Bxd3 9.Qxd3 e6 10.Nbd2 Bd6 11.Bxd6 { We have to exchange so we don’t lose time.
​ } 11...Qxd6 12.O-O O-O 13.Rfe1 Nd7 ( 13...Nh5 14.a3 f6 15.Rad1 Rae8 16.Qe3 g5 17.Nh2 Nf4 18.g3 Ng6 19.b4 ) ( 13...a6 14.Ne5 b5 15.Qf3 Nd7 16.Nxd7 Qxd7 17.Re3 Qd6 18.Rae1 b4 19.h4 bxc3 20.bxc3 Rab8 21.h5 Rb2 22.Nb3 Rxa2 23.h6 Qe7 24.Nc5 Rb2 25.Nd7 Rd8 ( 25...Qxd7 26.Qf6 ) 26.Nf6+ Kh8 27.Nxd5 Rxd5 28.Qxd5 exd5 29.Rxe7 Nxe7 30.Rxe7 Rb8 31.Rxf7 Ra8 32.Rd7 Kg8 33.Rxd5 a5 34.Rb5 a4 35.Rb2 a3 36.Ra2 Kf7 37.Kf1 Ke6 38.Ke2 Kd5 39.Kd3 g5 40.c4+ Ke6 41.Kc3 Kf5 42.Kb4 Ke4 43.Rxa3 Rxa3 44.Kxa3 Kxd4 45.Kb4 Ke5 46.Kb5 Kd6 47.Kb6 Kd7 48.c5 Kc8 49.Kc6 Kb8 50.Kd7 Kb7 51.c6+ ) ( 13...Rac8 14.Qe3 Nd7 15.Qh6 Rfe8 16.Re2 b5 17.a3 a5 18.Rae1 Qf8 19.Qf4 Qg7 20.Nf1 Nb6 21.N1h2 Nc4 22.Ng4 Kh8 23.Ng5 Rf8 24.g3 b4 25.axb4 axb4 26.Ra1 f5 27.Nh2 bxc3 28.bxc3 Nd8 29.Nxe6 Nxe6 30.Rxe6 g5 31.Qc1 Rf7 32.Qe1 Rcf8 33.g4 fxg4 34.Nxg4 Rf4 35.Raa6 Qb7 36.Nf6 Qg7 37.Re7 Re4 38.Qxe4 dxe4 39.Rxg7 Kxg7 40.Nxe4 h6 ) 14.a3 { We don’t have a clear plan, just improve everything until black commits to something. } 14...a6 15.Re2 b5 16.Rae1 Ne7 17.Ne5 Nxe5 18.Rxe5 Nf5 19.Nb3 Rfc8 20.g4 Ng7 21.h4 f6 22.R5e2 Qf4 23.Qh3 Re8 24.Nc5 a5 25.Nd3 Qc7 26.h5 { In this line we try to force the g-file open to start a kingside attack. Black wasted some time shuffling pieces and weakened their kingside pawns, so we pawnstorm. This is fine because the center is quite closed, as long as we keep discouraging e5. } 26...gxh5 27.gxh5 h6 28.Kh1 Qf7 29.Rg1 Kh7 30.Rg6 Nf5 31.Nf4 Ra6 ( 31...Ng7 32.Re1 Rg8 33.Qd3 f5 34.Qh3 Rae8 35.Reg1 Qe7 36.Nd3 Ref8 37.Ne5 Ne8 38.Qg3 f4 39.Qg4 Nf6 40.Qxf4 Rxg6 41.Nxg6 Qg7 42.Nxf8+ Qxf8 43.Rg6 Nd7 44.Qe3 Nf6 45.Qf3 Nd7 46.Qd3 Qf5 47.Qxf5 exf5 48.Rd6 ) 32.Rxe6 Raxe6 33.Qxf5 Re5 34.dxe5 Rxe5 35.Qd3 Rxh5+ 36.Nxh5 Qxg6 37.Nxf6+ Kg7 38.Qxg6+ Kxg6 39.Nxd5 ) 7.dxe5 { Give them an IQP (isolated queen’s pawn). Not many Caro-Kann players are comfortable playing this type of position, and our game plan will be to stop its advance first (plan 1). Once the pawn is frozen, we will then attack it (plan 2). } 7...Nxe5 8.Bc2 { Dont give away your bishop, nor allow Bf5. } 8...Bc5 { Develops a piece to the most active place. } ( 8...Qe7 { Trying to set up some double checks. } 9.Ne2 { Stops tactics with discovered attacks and double checks, while continuing to execute plan 1. } 9...Be6 10.Nd4 { Plan1 } 10...Nc6 11.O-O Nxd4 12.Qxd4 Qc5 { Smart move: exchanging queens with QxQ would help develop Black’s bishop, and allowing …QxQ would lead to cxd4 with a symmetrical position where Black’s isolated queen’s pawn becomes unexploitable because the d-file is blocked and we share the same weakness. } 13.Ba4+ Nd7 14.Be3 Qxd4 15.Bxd4 f6 16.Nd2 Kf7 { Gets the king to safety and unpins, trying to simplify the position. } 17.Nf3 Nc5 18.Bc2 Be7 19.Rfe1 { Plan 0 } 19...a5 20.Re2 b5 21.Rae1 Ra6 { doesnt blunder Bxc5 Bxc5, Rxe6 } 22.a3 { Stops further pawn advancement and sets up a deep idea with Bb1 and Ba2, including potential sacrificial themes that will appear in later lines. } 22...a4 23.Bb1 Bd6 24.Ba2 g5 ( 24...Rd8 25.Be3 { A knight is the best blockader of a pawn, so the plan is to maneuver our knight to block the isolated queen’s pawn.
​ } 25...d4 { This tries to give up the weakness in exchange for activity and complications, making our opponent believe they are winning a piece by attacking our bishop on a2 with a discovered attack from the bishop on e4 while also attacking our bishop on e3. No way White “blundered” this. Maybe black thinks this wins a piece. } 26.Bxe6+ { Woops, this comes with check, and we finally pick up the weak pawn. } 26...Nxe6 27.Nxd4 Nxd4 28.Bxd4 ) 25.Bxc5 { Eliminates a defender of the e6 square. } 25...Bxc5 26.Rxe6 { !! Brilliant sacrifice (i believe, didnt check). It contains a deep idea that fully justifies our bishop on a2. } 26...Rxe6 27.Bxd5 Re8 { Defends the rook so it is not simply blundered away. } 28.g4 { This looks random but has a very deep idea, trust me. The black position is almost completely paralyzed; Black’s only useful plan is …f5 and …Kf6 to unpin and try to free the position, but this move takes that idea away because …f5 is met by gxf5, and the rook is lost for less than a bishop. The goal is to paralyze the position fully, allowing us to keep improving our pieces and choose the most favorable moment to break the paralysis. } 28...Bb6 { black just doesnt have a usefull move. } 29.Nd4 { Attack an indefensible piece. } 29...Bxd4 { This move is forced because the rook is indefensible. } 30.cxd4 Re7 { Black has no useful moves and is reduced to shuffling, while we can continue improving our position to the fullest. } 31.Kg2 { Improve the king as much as possible. } 31...Re8 32.Kf3 Re7 33.Re4 { Our king wants to move to the center, but it cannot pass through a check, so the rook must shield it from the pinned rook. } 33...Re8 34.Ke3 Re7 35.Kd3 Re8 36.Rxe6 { Now this is the most favorable moment to exchange: our king has reached maximum activity, and pushing it further would only allow checks that create unnecessary complications. } 36...Rxe6 37.Kc3 { You probably wanted to capture immediately, but instead you should further activate your king and let him waste a move on breaking the pin and then recapturing, rather than just recapturing at once. This way you effectively win a free tempo. } 37...Ke7 38.Bxe6 Kxe6 39.Kb4 Kd5 40.Kxb5 Kxd4 41.Kxa4 { It takes Black around 10 moves to make a queen on the kingside, while we need only about 5 on the queenside, so Black is effectively trying to defend the indefensible while a completely symmetrical kingside structure ensures no counterplay. This is winning: if Black looks for kingside counterplay, freeze the pawn structure, aim for zugzwang, and push your queenside majority toward promotion, while carefully avoiding stalemate! } ) ( 8...Bd7 { This tries to develop the bishop to c6 or b5, since the ideas with …Bf5 and …Bg4 have already failed. } 9.Nf3 Nxf3+ ( 9...Bd6 10.Nbd2 O-O 11.Nxe5 Bxe5 12.Nf3 { Trade our weak d2 knight for his strong e5 knight, gaining a tempo on the bishop. } 12...Re8 13.Nxe5 Rxe5+ 14.Be3 Bf5 15.Bxf5 Rxf5 16.O-O Qd7 17.Qd3 h5 18.Rae1 Ne4 19.Bd4 Rc8 20.Bxg7 { It looks weird at first sight, but we’re opening up the pin on the d5 pawn with a clearance sacrifice of our bishop. } 20...Kxg7 21.Rxe4 dxe4 22.Qxd7 Rcc5 23.Qd4+ Rfe5 24.Rd1 ) 10.Qxf3 Qe7+ 11.Be3 { block the check and develop a piece. } 11...O-O-O { A rare move, but we can continu development while attacking both the d-pawn and the king. } 12.O-O a6 13.Nd2 ) ( 8...g6 { A weird but logical move, it tries to develop the bishop to f5. He can’t do it right now, though, because it would lead to isolated d and h pawns, and doubled isolated f pawns. Another idea could be to develop the dark-squared bishop to g7 and then try to make d4 happen that way. It takes one move longer than Bc5, but the bishop can’t be hit by a knight to gain a tempo. } 9.Nf3 Nxf3+ 10.Qxf3 Bg7 11.Ba4+ Bd7 12.Qe2+ { Black’s pieces are tangled, and this check exploits that.

If Black plays Ne4, we meet it with Bxd7+ followed by f3. } 12...Qe7 13.Bxd7+ Nxd7 14.Qxe7+ Kxe7 { Black’s king will be stuck in the center with an isolated pawn. } 15.Be3 Rhe8 16.Nd2 Kd6 17.O-O-O { This is the most efficient way to get the rook onto the d-file and our king to semi-safety and semi-activity. } 17...Kc6 18.Nf3 { stop the d-pawn advancement and open up the rook. } 18...b5 19.Nd4+ Bxd4 20.Rxd4 { Now we have a bishop-for-knight advantage, and we’ve lifted our rook to double up on the semi-open d-file. } 20...Re4 21.Rhd1 Rxd4 22.Rxd4 ) ( 8...Bd6 9.Nf3 O-O 10.O-O Bd7 11.Nxe5 Bxe5 12.Nd2 d4 { It looks like we’ve allowed d4, the only plan we really needed to stop. } ( 12...Re8 13.Nf3 { Win a tempo or the bishop pair. } 13...Bc7 14.Bg5 { Pin the knight to destabilize the d-pawn. } 14...Bb5 15.Bd3 Bxd3 16.Qxd3 Re6 17.Rfd1 ) 13.Nf3 { Fork. } ) 9.Nf3 { Challenge the active knight and fight for d4. Freezing the pawn (Plan 1). } 9...Nxf3+ { Taking the knight to try and enable d4. } ( 9...O-O { Gets the king to safety, blunders the knight, but intends to win it back with …Re8. } 10.Nxe5 Re8 11.Bf4 Bd6 12.O-O Bxe5 13.Bxe5 Rxe5 14.Nd2 Bf5 ( 14...d4 15.Nf3 ) 15.Bxf5 Rxf5 16.Nf3 { Plan 1 } ) 10.Qxf3 O-O ( 10...d4 { This tries to exchange the biggest weakness in Black’s position, but it is not a very good move. } 11.O-O { This gets your king to safety, but more importantly, it frees your rook for offensive measures. } 11...O-O { Only move to survive; any other move loses to Re1 or cxd4, which both lead to a lot of activity and leave the black king stuck in the center. } ) 11.O-O h6 { Kings are safe; Black is just waiting for us to blunder, which we will obviously not allow him to get. 😉 } ( 11...d4 12.Rd1 { Pinning the pawn to freeze it again. } 12...Qb6 { Only move that doesnt just lose the pawn. } 13.b4 { Kicks the bishop and wins the d-pawn; although some squares and pawns are weakened, being a pawn up compensates for it, and the engine evaluates the position at about +0.6. } 13...Be7 14.Rxd4 { Take with the rook, dont damage your stucture. } ) 12.Nd2 { develop a piece (plan 0) } 12...Re8 13.Nb3 { This move takes control of the d4 square (plan 1) while attacking a bishop that should not be exchanged, thereby gaining a tempo. } 13...Bb6 14.Rd1 { Plan 1 and 2 } 14...a5 { Tries to gain space, activate the rook, and chase away our knight in order to sabotage plan 1. } 15.Nd4 { Plan 1 } 15...a4 16.Be3 Ra5 { Activates the rook and defends the weakness. } 17.a3 { Stops further advancement of the a-pawn and sets up a beautiful plan with Bb1 and Ba2 that will appear later. } 17...Bd7 18.Re1 Ne4 19.Rad1 Ra6 20.Nf5 Bxe3 21.Rxe3 Bxf5 22.Qxf5 g6 23.Qg4 { Most active square for our queen. Attacking the h6 pawn was possible, but that would be a useless dopamine move, since …h5, …Kg5, and …Kg7 can all defend it while improving Black’s position. } 23...Nf6 24.Qd4 { Activates the queen even more while carrying out plan 1 and plan 2. Threatening to take on a4. } 24...Rxe3 25.Qxe3 h5 26.Rd4 Re6 27.Qd2 { We have a bishop-for-knight advantage. Our opponent still has an isolated pawn. We can go for the Bb1–Ba2 manoeuvre. This stops all infiltration, and our king is safer. } *

