1.e4 { take the center } {[%cal Ge2e4]} 1...e6 2.b3 { Against the French, we go for the Papa Ticulat Gambit. We sack a pawn, get insane development, castle queenside super fast, and launch a strong kingside attack. A lot of games end with White checkmating Black in absolutely beautiful style. } 2...d5 ( 2...b6 { If our opponent plays e6, he is probably intending to go for a French setup, but he can also choose something like e6 and b6 as a flexible system. We cannot predict which line he’ll choose, so we just have to play good moves against e6 from this position and react to his setup on the board, not to what we think he “wants” to play. Luckily, our plan stays pretty much the same: we go for Bb2, Nc3, Qe2, then push g4 and g5 to attack on the kingside and gain space. This setup works well against both the French-style structures and the e6–b6 systems, because we develop smoothly and then launch a strong pawn storm on the kingside. } 3.Bb2 Bb7 4.Nc3 d5 ( 4...c5 5.d4 cxd4 6.Qxd4 Nc6 7.Qd2 Nf6 8.O-O-O Bb4 9.f3 O-O 10.a3 Be7 11.h4 { This position looks very similar to our previous one, so we can stick to the same plan. } ) ( 4...Nf6 5.e5 Nd5 6.Nxd5 Bxd5 7.d4 Be7 8.Nh3 O-O 9.Nf4 Bb7 10.Bd3 g6 { This position looks a lot like that French line where Black played Nf6 on move 3 and we had to push e5 to kick the knight away. The pawn chain and piece placement are very similar, so the same strategic ideas and plans will often apply here as well. } 11.h4 Bxh4 12.Qg4 g5 13.Qh5 f5 14.exf6 Rf7 15.d5 e5 16.Bxe5 Qf8 17.Rxh4 gxh4 18.O-O-O d6 19.Qg5+ Kh8 20.Ng6+ Kg8 ( 20...hxg6 21.Qxh4+ Kg8 22.Bxg6 Rd7 23.f7+ Rxf7 ( 23...Qxf7 24.Qh8# ) 24.Qh8# ) 21.Nxh4+ Kh8 22.Bxh7 Kxh7 23.Nf5 Qh6 24.Qxh6+ Kg8 25.Rh1 Rh7 26.Qxh7+ Kf8 27.Qe7+ Kg8 28.f7# ) ( 4...Bb4 5.Qg4 { We instantly exploited the weakness, just like in those copycat Vienna lines where mirroring moves backfires and you can punish the opponent right away. } 5...Qf6 ( 5...Nf6 6.Qxg7 Rg8 7.Qh6 Nxe4 8.Qxh7 Nf6 9.Qh4 Be7 10.O-O-O ) 6.O-O-O Qxf2 ( 6...Nc6 7.Nd5 Qxf2 8.Nh3 { See how this is similar to a copycat Vienna. } 8...Qc5 9.Nxc7+ Kd8 10.Nxa8 Nge7 11.Kb1 Ng6 ) 7.Qxg7 Qf6 { This move looks like it saves the rook, but there is a rare tactic hidden in the position, take a moment and look for forcing moves like checks, captures, or threats that hit two pieces at once. } 8.Nb5 Qxg7 9.Bxg7 Kd8 10.Bxh8 ) 5.exd5 exd5 6.Qf3 { Our queen goes to a unique square this time, but the core idea hasn’t changed: we still want to go for g4 and g5 and launch a strong kingside pawn storm against his king. } 6...Nf6 7.O-O-O Be7 ( 7...Nc6 8.Bb5 ) 8.g4 ) 3.Bb2 dxe4 ( 3...Nf6 4.e5 Ne4 ( 4...Nfd7 5.Qg4 c5 6.f4 { Lock the center and start a kingside pawn storm. } 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 g6 8.Be2 Bg7 9.Nc3 a6 10.a4 O-O 11.h4 h5 12.Qh3 ) 5.d3 Nc5 6.d4 Ncd7 7.Bd3 { This now looks like a very nice kind of Advanced French structure. We just develop our pieces and go straight for a kingside attack. } 7...Be7 8.Qg4 O-O 9.Qh3 f5 10.g4 c5 11.gxf5 exf5 12.e6 Nf6 13.Bxf5 ) ( 3...d4 { It’s a rare move that takes us into pretty unique territory. We won’t be able to develop as easily as in the other lines, but we’ll still end up with a great position. } 4.Nf3 Nc6 ( 4...c5 5.c3 { We can’t really gain more development by just attacking the d-pawn, so we trade it off instead. That way we get our knight out and open up our bishop at the same time. } 5...Nc6 6.Bb5 dxc3 7.Bxc6+ bxc6 8.dxc3 { Actually, taking with the pawn is better. We can trade queens and head into a very favorable endgame, where his pawn structure is a total mess. Just develop, freeze his pawns in place, and then start targeting them one by one. } 8...Qxd1+ 9.Kxd1 Nf6 10.Nbd2 { This is a really important move. It defends the e-pawn, but the deeper point is that it stops c4, where he could even sack a pawn just to wreck our pawn structure. } ) 5.Bb5 Bc5 { We just keep developing while putting pressure on the d-pawn. } 6.c3 { He poured all his energy into defending the d-pawn, so now we just trade it off and cash in the free development. } 6...dxc3 7.Bxc6+ { Damage his pawn structure. } 7...bxc6 8.Nxc3 Nf6 9.d4 { We have the better structure, more development, and more central control. We also gain a tempo by hitting the bishop, and his a- and c-pawns are easy targets. Fantastic position. One simple middlegame plan is to win the c-pawns, then use your a- and b-pawns as a queenside majority against his lone a-pawn, create a passed pawn, and roll it down the board to promote. } 9...Bb4 10.Qd3 ) 4.Nc3 Nf6 ( 4...f5 { This move weakens his king just to save a pawn. We’ll focus on developing our pieces while he keeps shoving pawns around and creating holes around his king. } 5.d3 Nf6 ( 5...exd3 6.Bxd3 Nf6 7.Qe2 Be7 8.O-O-O ) 6.dxe4 Qxd1+ 7.Rxd1 Nxe4 8.Nxe4 fxe4 9.Rd4 Nc6 10.Rxe4 { We got the pawn back, we still have a clear target, and we have better development. Perfect position to test your endgame skills. } ) 5.Qe2 { You’ll reach this position in your games almost every time.
We basically always castle long and attack. Not much commentary needed here. Except for Qd4 and Nc6, the plan is always the same, castle long, go g4, g5, and if he plays h6, we hit him with h4. Learn the lines after that for your middlegame plans and tactics, and study all the typical checkmating patterns in these structures so you actually spot them over the board. } 5...Bb4 ( 5...Be7 6.O-O-O O-O 7.g4 Nc6 ( 7...h6 8.h4 { We will get our pawn storm in, no worries there. Make sure you learn the different ways we can attack from this structure so you don’t miss any brutal ideas. } 8...Nd5 ( 8...Nc6 9.g5 Nd4 10.Qe1 Ng4 11.Nxe4 h5 12.f3 Ne5 13.f4 Ng4 14.Ne2 Nxe2+ 15.Bxe2 e5 16.Ng3 exf4 17.Nxh5 ) 9.g5 h5 10.Nxe4 g6 11.Ng3 Nc6 12.Nxh5 gxh5 13.Qxh5 e5 14.Bd3 f5 15.Qg6+ Kh8 16.Qh6+ Kg8 17.g6 Nf6 ( 17...Rf7 18.gxf7+ Kxf7 19.Nf3 e4 20.Rhg1 Bf8 ( 20...exd3 21.Qg6+ Kf8 22.Qg8# ) 21.Ng5+ Ke7 22.Qh7+ Kd6 23.Nf7+ ) 18.Bc4+ ) 8.g5 Nd4 { This is a rare but very sharp line that you actually have to learn properly. } 9.Qe3 Ng4 10.Qg3 f5 11.h3 Bd6 12.f4 exf3 13.Qh4 Ne2+ 14.Ncxe2 fxe2 15.hxg4 exd1=Q+ 16.Kxd1 h6 17.Nf3 Bf4 18.Bxg7 Bxg5 19.Nxg5 Qxg5 20.Qxg5 hxg5 21.Bxf8 Kxf8 22.Rh7 fxg4 23.Rxc7 g3 24.Ke2 e5 25.Kf3 Be6 26.Kxg3 Rc8 27.Rxc8+ Bxc8 28.Bh3 Bxh3 29.Kxh3 Kf7 30.Kg4 Kf6 31.a4 a5 32.c3 b6 33.b4 e4 34.bxa5 bxa5 35.c4 Ke5 36.Kxg5 Kd4 37.Kf5 Kxc4 38.Kxe4 Kb4 39.Kd5 Kxa4 40.Kc4 Ka3 41.d4 Kb2 42.d5 a4 43.d6 a3 44.d7 a2 45.d8=Q a1=Q 46.Qd4+ Kb1 47.Qd1+ Kb2 48.Qd2+ Kb1 49.Kb3 Qc3+ 50.Kxc3 Ka1 51.Qb2# ) ( 5...Qd4 6.f3 { This is the only line where we don’t castle long immediately. If he takes, we can recapture with tempo and open lines toward his king. It looks logical for lower-rated players because you’re “pinning” the knight to the bishop, but honestly, it’s a pretty bad move. } 6...exf3 7.Nxf3 Qd8 8.O-O-O { Now we get basically the same position, except the weak e-pawn is gone. In return, we’ve developed our knight, we’ve castled, and we have a semi-open f-file, which is fantastic compensation.Our plan stays the same, kingside pawn storm. Rg1 is the cleanest way to prepare g4 and start throwing pawns at his king. } ) ( 5...Nc6 6.O-O-O Nd4 ( 6...Be7 7.Nxe4 { We want to open up our bishop quickly so it can fight for control of d4, so we take the pawn. Other than that, everything stays the same. } 7...Nd4 8.Qe3 ) 7.Qe1 Be7 8.d3 exd3 9.Bxd3 { We again let him trade off his weak pawn in return for our development and open lines. Our rook is now activated and our bishops are doing a great job slicing through the position. } ) 6.O-O-O Qe7 ( 6...Bxc3 7.dxc3 { We open up the rook, and we can always reactivate our bishop later. } 7...Qe7 8.g4 O-O 9.g5 Nd5 10.Nh3 f5 11.gxf6 Nxf6 12.Ng5 { Now we have two targets. } 12...e5 13.Nxe4 Nxe4 14.Qxe4 Nc6 15.Rd2 Bf5 16.Qe3 Rad8 17.Bc4+ Kh8 18.Bd5 Qd6 19.c4 ) 7.g4 Ba3 8.g5 Bxb2+ 9.Kxb2 Nd5 10.h4 Bd7 11.Bg2 Nc6 12.Bxe4 O-O-O 13.Nf3 Kb8 14.Rdg1 Qd6 15.h5 a6 16.Qc4 Nb6 17.Qd3 Qxd3 18.Bxd3 *

