1.e4 { take the center } {[%cal Ge2e4]} 1...d5 2.exd5 Qxd5 ( 2...c6 3.dxc6 Nxc6 4.Nf3 { We’ve won a pawn, and we’ll have super fast development combined with a straight-up checkmating attack. } 4...e5 5.Nc3 Nf6 6.Bb5 ) ( 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 Nxd5 { We don’t care whether he takes with the knight or the queen, we just want to play for d4 and c4. We’ll get very similar lines either way, and the position will look basically the same. } 4.d4 Bg4 5.Be2 e6 6.O-O Be7 7.h3 Bh5 8.c4 Nf6 9.Nc3 Nc6 10.Be3 O-O 11.Re1 h6 12.a3 a5 13.Rc1 ) 3.Nf3 { This is a less well-known line that aims to kick the queen with d4 and c4 instead of the usual Nc3. It leads to really nice setups and fun attacking positions. } 3...Bg4 ( 3...Nf6 4.d4 e6 ( 4...c5 5.Nc3 Qd8 6.Bb5+ Bd7 7.dxc5 Bxb5 8.Qxd8+ Kxd8 9.Nxb5 { We’ve won a pawn, and we’ll have super fast development combined with a straight-up checkmating attack. } ) ( 4...Bg4 5.Be2 ) 5.c4 { We achieved d4 and c4. Now we’ll just develop behind this pawn duo and go for his king. } 5...Qa5+ 6.Bd2 Bb4 7.Nc3 O-O 8.Bd3 Nbd7 9.a3 Bxc3 10.Bxc3 Qh5 11.Qe2 Rb8 12.h3 b5 { All the lines are very straightforward, but this one is basically not findable over the board and still super sharp. So learn the ideas and tactics behind it. } 13.cxb5 Bb7 14.O-O Bxf3 15.Qxf3 Qxf3 16.gxf3 Nb6 17.a4 Rfd8 18.Rfc1 Nh5 19.Be4 Nf4 20.Kf1 Nxh3 21.Bd2 f5 22.a5 fxe4 23.axb6 axb6 24.Kg2 Rxd4 25.Be3 Nf4+ 26.Bxf4 exf3+ 27.Kg3 h5 28.Bxc7 Rf8 29.Ra7 Rf5 30.Bxb6 Rg4+ 31.Kh3 Rxb5 32.Rc8+ Kh7 33.Be3 Rxb2 34.Ra5 Kg6 35.Rxh5 Kxh5 36.Rh8+ Kg6 37.Kxg4 ) 4.Be2 Nc6 ( 4...Nf6 5.d4 e6 ( 5...Nc6 6.O-O { This baits him into a trap. } 6...Bxf3 ( 6...O-O-O 7.Be3 e5 ( 7...e6 8.h3 Bh5 ( 8...h5 9.Nbd2 ) 9.c4 Qd7 10.Nbd2 ) 8.c4 Qa5 9.d5 ) 7.Bxf3 Qxd4 8.Bxc6+ bxc6 9.Qxd4 ) 6.O-O Be7 7.h3 h5 { We almost transpose to the line where he sacs his bishop, but here we can actually take, as long as you know your lines. } 8.hxg4 hxg4 9.Ne5 Nc6 10.Nc3 ) 5.O-O O-O-O 6.h3 { We kick the bishop and he suddenly has a bunch of options. This variation leads to all kinds of sharp lines, so study this position and the ideas very carefully. } 6...Bxf3 ( 6...Bh5 { If he saves the bishop, we can go for a very aggressive queenside attack. Swapping a bishop for a knight is usually bad, but here his bishop will just get in the way of his kingside pawn storm and counterplay. } 7.Nc3 Qa5 ( 7...Qd7 8.b4 Nxb4 9.Ne5 Qf5 ( 9...Bxe2 10.Qxe2 Qe6 11.Rb1 Nc6 12.Qb5 Nxe5 13.Qxb7+ Kd7 14.d4 Ng6 15.d5 Qd6 16.Ne4 Qe5 17.Nc5+ Ke8 18.Qc6+ Rd7 19.Rb8# ) 10.Rb1 Nc6 11.Nxc6 bxc6 ) 8.b4 Qxb4 9.Rb1 Qd6 10.d4 Nf6 11.Nb5 Qe6 12.c4 Bxf3 13.Bxf3 Qxc4 14.Bf4 e5 15.Bxc6 bxc6 16.Nxa7+ Kd7 17.dxe5+ Ke8 18.exf6 Rxd1 19.Rfxd1 gxf6 20.Bxc7 f5 21.Re1+ Kd7 22.Be5 Rg8 23.Rb7+ Ke6 24.Bc3+ Kd6 25.Nc8+ Kc5 26.Re5+ Qd5 27.Rxd5+ cxd5 28.a4 Rg5 29.Rc7# ) ( 6...h5 { This is called a Fishing Pole sacrifice. We can’t take yet, but we’re getting ready for it. It’s a very sharp line, so study it well. } 7.c3 Nf6 ( 7...e5 8.hxg4 { e5 blocked his queen from transferring to the kingside, so now we can finally take. } 8...hxg4 9.Ng5 f5 10.Qb3 Qd6 11.Nf7 Qf6 12.Nxh8 Nh6 13.d3 Bd6 14.g3 Rxh8 15.Kg2 f4 16.Qd1 ) 8.d4 e6 9.Re1 Bd6 10.Nbd2 Qf5 11.Nf1 Bxh3 { It’s too dangerous to take the bishop, but we can trap the queen. } 12.Ne3 Qg6 13.Bd3 Bf5 14.Nxf5 exf5 15.Nh4 Qh7 16.Bxf5+ ) 7.Bxf3 Qd7 8.Re1 e6 9.c3 Nf6 10.d4 Bd6 11.Nd2 { We go for this kind of setup in a lot of these lines, and we’ll fight for the center and attack his king. } 11...g5 12.Nc4 Rhg8 13.Qe2 h5 14.Ne5 Nxe5 15.dxe5 g4 16.exd6 gxf3 17.Qxf3 Rg6 18.dxc7 Rdg8 19.Kh1 Rxg2 20.Bf4 R2g6 21.Rad1 Qb5 22.a4 Qxb2 23.Rb1 Qc2 24.Qxb7+ Kd7 25.c8=Q# *

