1.d4 { WELCOME TO THE COLLE SYSTEM.

WHY THIS OPENING IS PERFECT FOR YOU RIGHT NOW:
You play almost the same seven moves in every single game. You do not need to memorise twenty different openings. You do not need to know what your opponent plays. You set up your pieces the same way, and then you attack.

Most players your level lose games in the opening because they do not know what to do. After this course, you will always know what to do.

THE SEVEN MOVES YOU MUST KNOW BY HEART:
d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3, Nbd2, O-O, and c3 (only when needed).
That is it. Learn these and you have an opening for life.

WHAT YOU WILL LEARN IN THIS COURSE:
- Lesson 1: The setup and the plan
- Lesson 2: The e4 break (this is how you win)
- Lesson 3: The bishop sacrifice on h7 (the knockout punch)
- Lesson 4-6: What to do when Black plays something else
- Lesson 7: The three mistakes that cost you the game

TIME NEEDED: about two hours. Then start playing it immediately. } 1...d5 2.Nf3 Nf6 3.e3 { This modest pawn move is the whole opening. It opens the diagonal for your light-squared bishop.

IMPORTANT: your dark-squared bishop on c1 is now blocked. That is the price you pay. Do not worry about it - that bishop usually comes out later via b2 or after you push e4. } 3...e6 ( 3...Bg4 { Black develops his bishop OUTSIDE the pawn chain before playing ...e6. This is actually a good idea against the Colle, so do not play on autopilot here.

DO NOT PLAY 4.Bd3? - Black gets a very comfortable game with ...Nbd7 and ...e5. } 4.h3 { ASK THE QUESTION. Make the bishop decide right away. } 4...Bh5 { If instead 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 you get the two bishops and a pleasant game - that is already a small success. } 5.g4 { Bold, but correct. You gain space and start hunting the bishop. Your king is fine because the centre is closed. } 5...Bg6 6.Ne5 { THE POINT. The knight is strong here and you threaten to trap or trade the bishop with h4-h5. } 6...Nfd7 { Black's best - he challenges your knight. } 7.Nxg6 hxg6 8.c4 { Be happy: you own the bishop pair and more space. Do not chase more - just develop and use your advantages.

YOUR PLAN: Nc3, Qb3 hitting b7 and d5, and later castle on either side. Your extra bishop will tell in the long run. } ) 4.Bd3 { THE COLLE BISHOP. Look at where it points: straight at h7, next to Black's king. Almost every win in this opening ends with a sacrifice on that square. Never trade this bishop cheaply. } 4...c5 ( 4...Be7 { You will see this a LOT at club level. Black develops sensibly but does not challenge your centre with ...c5. } 5.O-O O-O 6.Nbd2 Nbd7 7.e4 { PUNISH IT IMMEDIATELY. Because Black never played ...c5, nothing attacks your d4-pawn, so you do not need c3 at all.

This is the biggest practical tip in the whole course: no ...c5 from Black means no c3 from you, and e4 comes for free. } 7...dxe4 8.Nxe4 Nxe4 9.Bxe4 Nf6 10.Bd3 { Retreat and keep aiming at h7. Your position is simply better: more space, better bishops, easier plan. } 10...c5 11.dxc5 Bxc5 12.Bg5 { Pin the knight. Now watch what happens if Black is careless. } 12...Be7 13.Qe2 Qc7 14.Rad1 Rd8 15.Ne5 Bd7 16.Bxh7+ { There it is again. Same sacrifice, different move order. } 16...Kxh7 17.Bxf6 Bxf6 18.Qh5+ Kg8 19.Qxf7+ { White wins. Notice: the h7 sacrifice appears in almost every lesson. That is not a coincidence - it is the soul of this opening. } ) ( 4...b6 { Black plans ...Bb7, which fights for the e4-square. Now the e4 break does not work well.

WHEN PLAN A FAILS, SWITCH TO PLAN B. } 5.O-O Bb7 6.Ne5 { PLAN B: THE STONEWALL. Put the knight on the strong e5-square and back it up with f4. Instead of breaking in the centre, you attack directly on the kingside. } 6...Bd6 7.Nd2 O-O 8.f4 { The Stonewall is built. Your pawn on f4 supports the knight and your rook can swing to f3. } 8...c5 9.Qf3 { Useful move: it defends against tricks and prepares to bring the queen to the kingside via h3 or g3. } 9...Qc8 10.c3 Ba6 11.Bxa6 Nxa6 12.g4 { THE ATTACK. Push the g-pawn and open lines toward the black king. This is a completely different kind of game than the e4 lines - and often easier to play at club level, because your plan is so simple: throw pawns at the king. } 12...Ne8 13.Qg2 { White has space and a clear attacking plan.

WHEN TO USE THE STONEWALL: whenever Black stops e4 with ...b6 and ...Bb7, or plays a very solid setup with ...Be7. If e4 does not work, go for Ne5 and f4. } ) 5.c3 { NOW we play c3, because Black attacks d4 and we need to defend it.

GOLDEN RULE: play c3 only when Black plays ...c5. If you play c3 too early you waste a move. } 5...Nc6 ( 5...Nbd7 { Black's other main setup: the knight goes to d7 instead of c6.

ONE THING CHANGES FOR YOU: do NOT play dxc5 here. Instead play Re1. } 6.Nbd2 Bd6 7.O-O O-O 8.Re1 { THE ROOK MOVE. Remember this: knight on c6 means dxc5, knight on d7 means Re1.

The rook backs up the e-pawn and gets ready for something special. } 8...Qc7 9.e4 cxd4 10.cxd4 dxe4 11.Nxe4 Nxe4 12.Rxe4 { TAKE WITH THE ROOK, not the bishop. This is the whole point of Re1. } 12...Nf6 13.Rh4 { THE ROOK LIFT. Your rook swings sideways to the h-file and joins the attack on the black king.

This looks strange the first time you see it. It is completely normal in this opening. The rook attacks h7 together with your bishop. } 13...Be7 14.Bf4 { Develop with tempo, hitting the queen. } 14...Qa5 15.a3 g6 16.Rc1 Nd5 17.Bg5 { Your pieces all point at the black king. Typical follow-up: Bh6 and then a sacrifice on g6. } 17...f6 18.Bh6 Rd8 19.Qc2 { Queen joins. Black is in serious trouble.

REMEMBER THE PATTERN: Re1, e4, take with the ROOK, Rh4, bishops to f4 and g5-h6, queen to c2. } ) 6.Nbd2 { Both knights are out. Notice the knight goes to d2, not c3 - it must not block the c-pawn. } 6...Bd6 7.O-O { King is safe. Development is finished. Now the second half of the plan starts: the attack. } 7...O-O 8.dxc5 { THE KEY MOVE OF THE WHOLE COURSE. Trade on c5 FIRST, then play e4.

WHY? Two reasons:
1. Black's pressure on your d4-pawn disappears.
2. His bishop lands on c5 where it does nothing, and your e4 push comes with tempo.

Players who skip this move and rush e4 usually get a worse position. } ( 8.e4 { MISTAKE 1: PUSHING e4 TOO EARLY.

You skipped dxc5. Now Black can meet e4 with ...cxd4 and ...dxe4, and after the dust settles you often end up with an isolated pawn on d4 and no attack. Always trade on c5 first.

The correct move here is 8.dxc5. } 8...cxd4 9.cxd4 dxe4 10.Nxe4 { White is fine here but has nothing special - and this is the BEST case. Compare it to Lesson 1 where you were mating.

MISTAKE 2: TRADING YOUR LIGHT-SQUARED BISHOP.
Never allow a cheap trade of your d3-bishop. If Black plays ...Ba6 or ...Bg4 threatening to trade, think twice. That bishop is your main attacker.

MISTAKE 3: ATTACKING WITH TOO FEW PIECES.
Before you sacrifice on h7, count your attackers. You need at least: the bishop, a knight ready for g5, and the queen able to reach h5 or g4. Two attacking pieces is not enough - the sacrifice will simply lose a piece.

CHECKLIST BEFORE EVERY Bxh7+ SACRIFICE:
1. Has Black's knight left f6?
2. Can my knight reach g5 with check?
3. Can my queen reach h5 or g4 in one move?
Three times yes = go. Any no = keep building. } ) 8...Bxc5 9.e4 { THE BREAK. Everything you did was preparation for this one move. The centre opens, your bishop on d3 comes alive, and the attack begins.

Now go to Lesson 2. } 9...Qc7 { Black's most natural move. He defends and prepares to play ...e5 himself. } 10.Qe2 { Your queen supports the pawn push e4-e5 and eyes the kingside. Simple and strong. } 10...Re8 ( 10...h6 { Stronger players will play this. The pawn on h6 takes away g5 and stops the bishop sacrifice.

DO NOT PANIC. You do not need a sacrifice to win. You have a healthy position with more space. } 11.Bc2 { Step aside and keep the bishop pointing at h7. Your plan now is Qd3, lining up queen and bishop on the same diagonal. } 11...Rd8 12.e5 { Gain space and kick the knight. } 12...Ng4 13.Nb3 Bb6 14.Bf4 { Develop and protect the e5-pawn. Notice how your last bishop finally came out - the one that was blocked in on move 3. } 14...f6 15.Qd3 { There it is: queen and bishop aiming at h7 together. Even without a sacrifice, this pressure is unpleasant to face. } 15...f5 16.h3 Nf6 17.Nbd4 { A roughly equal position - but much easier to play with White. You have space, a clear plan, and Black must defend precisely.

YOUR PLAN FROM HERE: play on the kingside with g4, or improve your knights via d4 and f3 to e5. } ) 11.e5 { Kick the knight away from the kingside. With the defender gone, Black's king is in danger. } 11...Nd7 12.Nb3 { Chase away the annoying bishop on c5. Small move, big idea: it clears the way and gains time. } 12...Bf8 13.Bxh7+ { THE GREEK GIFT. This is the sacrifice you came here for. Learn this pattern - it wins games at every level.

WHY IT WORKS HERE (check these three things every time):
1. Black's knight has left f6, so nothing guards h7.
2. Your knight can jump to g5 with check.
3. Your queen can join the attack quickly.

If all three are true, the sacrifice usually works. } 13...Kxh7 14.Ng5+ { Check! And your queen is ready to come to h5 or g4. } 14...Kg6 { The only move. If 14...Kg8 15.Qh5 and mate follows quickly. } 15.f4 { Quiet but deadly. You threaten f5+ and the black king has nowhere to run. } 15...f5 16.Qf3 Nf6 17.Qg3 Nh5 18.Nxe6+ { Winning material with check. } 18...Nxg3 19.Nxc7 Re7 20.Nxa8 Nxf1 21.Kxf1 { White has won material and the game. You do not need to memorise this far - just remember the pattern: e5, Nb3, Bxh7+, Ng5+, and bring the queen. } *

