WHY THIS OPENING IS PERFECT FOR YOU RIGHT NOW:
You play almost the same seven moves in every single game. You do not need to memorise twenty different openings. You do not need to know what your opponent plays. You set up your pieces the same way, and then you attack.

Most players your level lose games in the opening because they do not know what to do. After this course, you will always know what to do.

THE SEVEN MOVES YOU MUST KNOW BY HEART:
d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3, Nbd2, O-O, and c3 (only when needed).
That is it. Learn these and you have an opening for life.

WHAT YOU WILL LEARN IN THIS COURSE:
- Lesson 1: The setup and the plan
- Lesson 2: The e4 break (this is how you win)
- Lesson 3: The bishop sacrifice on h7 (the knockout punch)
- Lesson 4-6: What to do when Black plays something else
- Lesson 7: The three mistakes that cost you the game

TIME NEEDED: about two hours. Then start playing it immediately. } ) 1...c6 { most likely the caro-kann } ( 1...c5 ) ( 1...d5 ) ( 1...e5 ) 2.d4 { take the whole center if given to you } ( 2.Nc3 d5 3.Nf3 ( 3.exd5 cxd5 4.d4 ) 3...Bg4 4.h3 Bxf3 5.Qxf3 e6 6.d4 Nf6 7.e5 ( 7.Bd3 dxe4 8.Nxe4 Qxd4 9.Be3 Bb4+ 10.c3 ( 10.Ke2 Qe5 11.Bf4 Qd5 12.Nxf6+ gxf6 13.Be4 Qc4+ ) 10...Qxd3 11.Nxf6+ gxf6 12.cxb4 Nd7 13.Rd1 Qa6 14.Rd4 O-O-O 15.a4 Ne5 16.Qxf6 Nd3+ 17.Kf1 Rxd4 18.Bxd4 Rd8 19.Kg1 Qxa4 ) ( 7.Be3 Bb4 8.Bd3 dxe4 9.Bxe4 Nxe4 10.Qxe4 O-O 11.O-O Nd7 12.a3 Nf6 13.Qd3 Bd6 ) 7...Nfd7 8.Bd3 c5 9.Be3 Nc6 10.Ne2 c4 ) ( 2.Nf3 d5 3.exd5 ( 3.e5 Bg4 4.d4 e6 5.Be2 c5 6.Be3 Bxf3 7.Bxf3 cxd4 8.Bxd4 Nc6 9.O-O Nh6 10.c4 Qh4 11.Bc3 Qxc4 12.Be2 Qc5 13.Nd2 Qb6 14.a3 Bc5 15.Rc1 O-O 16.h3 Bd4 17.Nf3 Bxc3 18.bxc3 Rac8 ) ( 3.Nc3 ) 3...cxd5 4.d4 ( 4.c4 d4 5.g3 Nc6 6.Bg2 Nf6 7.d3 e5 8.O-O Nd7 9.Bd2 a5 10.Na3 Bxa3 11.bxa3 O-O 12.Rb1 Nc5 ) ) ( 2.c4 d5 3.exd5 ( 3.cxd5 cxd5 4.e5 Nc6 5.d4 Bf5 6.Nf3 ( 6.a3 e6 7.Nc3 a6 8.Be3 f6 9.Nf3 fxe5 10.dxe5 Nh6 11.Bxh6 gxh6 12.Bd3 Bg4 13.Be2 Bc5 14.O-O O-O ) 6...e6 7.Nc3 Nge7 8.Bg5 Qb6 9.Bb5 a6 10.Bxc6+ Nxc6 11.O-O ( 11.Qd2 h6 12.Be3 Na5 13.Na4 Qb5 14.Nc3 Qb4 15.b3 Rc8 16.Rc1 Rc7 17.O-O Nc6 18.Nh4 Bh7 19.h3 Be7 20.Nf3 O-O 21.Na4 Qxd2 22.Bxd2 Ba3 23.Rc3 b5 24.Nc5 Ra8 25.Nb7 Bb2 26.Rc5 Rac8 27.Nd6 Nxd4 28.Nxd4 Rxc5 29.Nxc8 Rxc8 ) 11...Qxb2 12.Rc1 h6 13.Bd2 Ba3 14.Re1 Qb6 ) 3...cxd5 4.cxd5 ( 4.d4 ) 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 Nxd5 6.Nf3 ( 6.d4 g6 ) ( 6.Qb3 Nb6 7.Bb5+ Bd7 8.d4 Bxb5 9.Qxb5+ Nc6 10.d5 a6 11.Qb3 Nd4 12.Qd1 e5 13.dxe6 Nxe6 14.Qxd8+ Rxd8 ) ( 6.Bc4 Nb6 7.Bb3 g6 8.d4 ) 6...g6 7.d4 ) ( 2.Bc4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bb5+ Bd7 5.Qe2 ( 5.Bxd7+ Qxd7 6.d4 e6 7.Nf3 Bd6 8.O-O Ne7 9.Ne5 Bxe5 10.dxe5 Nbc6 11.f4 Nf5 12.Nc3 ) 5...e6 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.d4 Bd6 8.O-O Nge7 9.Nbd2 O-O 10.Re1 Qc7 11.c3 f6 12.Nf1 e5 13.dxe5 fxe5 14.Bxc6 Nxc6 15.Rd1 e4 16.Nd4 Nxd4 17.Rxd4 Bc5 ) 2...d5 { now we are sure its the caro-khan } 3.e5 ( 3.exd5 { Theoretically speaking, the advanced variation is the best, but your opponent will be very comfortable.
We are smarter and play THY Lanckriet System, which constantly forces our opponent into very uncomfortable decisions. } 3...cxd5 { Our opponent wants a Caro-Kann, but we’ve got something devious prepared that’s going to make him very uncomfortable. } 4.Nf3 ( 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.Nf3 ) ( 4.Bd3 { We play against the bishop on c8.
As Magnus Carlsen said, “Pick a piece and play against it.”
We will make it very difficult for the bishop to develop.
Our bishop controls the f5 square, and our queen prevents Bg4. } 4...Nc6 5.c3 { Dont blunder your d poin. } 5...Nf6 { This intends to prepare the development of the bishop to g4. } 6.h3 { Stop the plan. } ( 6.Bf4 Bg4 7.Qb3 Qd7 8.Nd2 e6 9.Ngf3 Bd6 10.Bxd6 ( 10.Ne5 Qc7 11.Nxg4 Bxf4 12.Nxf6+ gxf6 ) 10...Qxd6 11.Qxb7 ( 11.O-O O-O 12.Rae1 ( 12.Rfe1 Qc7 13.Ne5 Bh5 14.Ndf3 Rab8 ) 12...Rab8 13.Ne5 Rfc8 14.Nxg4 Nxg4 15.Nf3 b5 16.h3 ( 16.Bxb5 a6 ) 16...Nf6 17.Ne5 b4 ) 11...Rb8 12.Qa6 O-O 13.Rb1 ( 13.Bb5 Rb6 14.Qa4 Ne7 ) 13...Rb6 14.Qa4 e5 15.dxe5 Nxe5 16.Be2 Nxf3+ 17.Nxf3 Re8 ) 6...e5 { This fights for the center, opens it up, and ensures that the bishop does not get stuck behind a light-squared pawn chain. It is the best move, but very uncomfortable for a Caro-Kann player. } ( 6...e6 ) 7.dxe5 { Give them an IQP (isolated queen’s pawn). Not many Caro-Kann players are comfortable playing this type of position, and our game plan will be to stop its advance first (plan 1). Once the pawn is frozen, we will then attack it (plan 2). } 7...Nxe5 8.Nf3 ( 8.Qe2 Ne4 9.Bxe4 dxe4 10.Qxe4 Bd6 11.f4 ( 11.Nf3 O-O 12.Nxe5 ( 12.O-O Nxf3+ 13.Qxf3 Bd7 14.Qxb7 Rc8 15.Qxa7 Bb8 16.Qe3 Rc6 17.Nd2 Rg6 18.Ne4 f5 19.Ng3 f4 20.Qd4 fxg3 21.f3 Qc7 22.Be3 Bxh3 23.gxh3 g2 24.Rf2 ( 24.Rfe1 Qh2+ 25.Kf2 g1=Q# ) 24...Qh2# ) 12...Bxe5 13.Qxe5 Re8 ) 11...Qh4+ ) ( 8.Bc2 { Dont give away your bishop, nor allow Bf5. } 8...Bc5 { Develops a piece to the most active place. } 9.Nf3 { Challenge the active knight and fight for d4. Freezing the pawn (Plan 1). } 9...Nxf3+ { Taking the knight to try and enable d4. } 10.Qxf3 Qe7+ 11.Qe2 Qxe2+ 12.Kxe2 d4 13.Re1 O-O ) 8...Nxd3+ 9.Qxd3 Bd6 10.O-O O-O 11.Bg5 h6 12.Bxf6 ( 12.Bh4 g5 13.Nxg5 ( 13.Bg3 Bxg3 14.fxg3 g4 15.hxg4 Nxg4 ) 13...hxg5 14.Bxg5 Bc7 15.Qf3 ( 15.Bh4 Kg7 ) 15...Qd6 ) 12...Qxf6 13.Qxd5 Rd8 14.Qb3 ( 14.Nbd2 Bh2+ { A simple tactic. } ) 14...Bxh3 { A simple tactic. } 15.gxh3 Qxf3 ) ( 4.c4 Nf6 5.Nc3 g6 6.Qb3 ( 6.cxd5 Nxd5 7.Bc4 ( 7.Qb3 Nb6 8.Nf3 Be6 9.Bb5+ Nc6 10.Qd1 Bg